window.harnessChunks["rows-812bfe3d2d9a5e7c.js"]=["<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"4\" data-a=\"4\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-20T21:45:19+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/openags/auto-researcher\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">OpenAGS</a><small>2024</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"4\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>4</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-20\">2026-09-20</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/openags/auto-researcher/tree/9bb9dfc6d5e66ad92e3652c8a2ed250d62f8c11f\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 9bb9dfc6d5e66ad92e3652c8a2ed250d62f8c11f\">9bb9dfc &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: specialist research agents perform the operational scientific work: literature review, proposal development, experiments, manuscript production, and peer review/revision artifacts. - Disturbance / variety regulated: open-ended scientific questions, external literature, experiment implementation/execution, result interpretation, and manuscript construction. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the next specialist reasoning/tool/file action needed to produce each role&#39;s research outcome within its SOUL/skill contract. - Decision owner: each first-party builtin OpenAGS Agent executing the corresponding specialist folder/role. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: SOUL and skill loading, tool engine, memory/session/history, filesystem workspaces, upstream/downstream paths, LiteLLM transport, timeouts, and orchestrator lifecycle. - Closure path: observations/tool results return to the specialist Agent loop; the agent updates role artifacts/status and those outputs become available to downstream agents/coordinator. - Boundary reachability: the repository documents a complete first-party builtin Agent path (Agent.loop) used by the research layer; S1 therefore does not depend on Claude Code/Codex/Cursor/Gemini owning the operational loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the first-party agent engine closes model decision → tool execution → observation → next decision inside the supported builtin mode. - Evidence: builtin-agent execution path, folder-as-agent model, default specialist workflow. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: alternate CLI-agent backends are not used to justify S1 ownership; the credited mode is the self-contained builtin OpenAGS agent engine.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no material S2-specific regulation of a demonstrated inter-S1 conflict/oscillation is established at the declared project recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: not established. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established as S2. - Decision owner: not applicable. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: upstream/downstream file contracts, fixed first-pass sequence, TASKS/STATUS files, agent dispatch, and shared project artifacts organize work but do not by themselves establish an inter-S1 interference witness. - Closure path: workflow outputs feed downstream specialists, but that sequencing/dependency path is not sufficient for S2. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: named multi-agent roles and communication through files are generic organization/hand-off primitives unless tied to a concrete conflict or oscillation between S1 units and an attenuation loop. - Evidence: file-based agent communication, coordinator first-pass sequence. - Basis: structural negative finding. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: workflow dependency management may prevent some practical clashes, but the required S2-specific disturbance/attenuation witness is not established in the reviewed evidence.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: maintain a whole-project current view and decide which specialist work starts, waits, reruns, or is complete. - Disturbance / variety regulated: incomplete or failed stages, changing project status, downstream dependency readiness, review-identified weaknesses, and need to redirect current research effort. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the next active specialist/task and, after the first pass, choose which research stages must rerun in response to current project evidence. - Decision owner: the root coordinator Agent in Auto Mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: specialist STATUS.md and TASKS.md, workflow/orchestrator process management, timeout/failure handling, fixed initial stage order, project files, and start/wait/all-complete action protocol. - Closure path: coordinator reads current statuses/review findings, emits startagent/wait/allcomplete/needshuman decisions and writes/assigns specialist tasks; the orchestrator launches or withholds subsequent work accordingly. - Boundary reachability: Auto Mode and the root coordinator are part of the default first-party project template and builtin workflow, not an external operator or development-only controller. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic orchestration executes start/stop/status mechanics, while the coordinator Agent is explicitly instructed to make pipeline decisions and decide what happens next from the whole project state. - Whole-system current view: all specialist status files, project-level memory/key decisions, current stage completion, and review outputs across the research project. - Current-control decision scope: assignment and ordering of current specialist work, whether to wait, whether the project is complete, and which stages to rerun after review. - Evidence: coordinator Auto Mode and role, workflow protocol. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: fixed first-pass sequencing is enforcement/support; S3 credit comes from the coordinator&#39;s evidenced discretionary current-control decisions, especially status-based dispatch and post-review rerun choice.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independently review the manuscript/research claims using complementary access to manuscript, experiment results, literature notes, and external citation verification, then return actionable findings into project control. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unsupported claims, weak methodology/experiments, citation hallucination or misrepresentation, missing baselines/limitations, and paper-level weaknesses not caught by ordinary production. - Decisive decision or feedback right: make peer-review judgments, classify major/minor concerns, score review criteria, issue a verdict, and prescribe a revision roadmap. - Decision owner: the distinct review Agent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: dedicated review SOUL/folder, read access to manuscript/experiment/literature artifacts, web search for citation spot-checking, structured review format, and persisted review-report.md. - Closure path: review findings are written under review/reviews/; after the first pass the coordinator reads those weaknesses and decides which stages need to rerun, returning audit findings into changed subsequent operation. - Boundary reachability: the reviewer is a default specialist in the standard project template and a standard first-pass stage; its report is explicitly part of the coordinator&#39;s iteration mode. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the reviewer makes substantive adversarial/audit judgments rather than merely applying a deterministic syntax gate; the coordinator, not the reviewer, owns corrective project-control decisions. - Claim being audited: the manuscript&#39;s scientific claims, citations, experimental support, logic, completeness, and reproducibility. - Ordinary reporting path: manuscript output and specialist status/results flow normally into the project filesystem and coordinator. - Complementary access path: the reviewer reads the full manuscript plus experiment report plus literature review and independently web-searches sampled citations; it is instructed to adversarially probe alternative explanations/failure conditions/missing experiments. - Independence boundary: a separate review role/folder with its own SOUL and artifact is outside the manuscript-producing agent&#39;s ordinary production loop, while retaining direct access to underlying experiment/literature evidence. - Who acts on findings: the root coordinator reads review weaknesses and selects which stages to rerun; affected specialists then perform the corrective work. - Evidence: reviewer SOUL, coordinator iteration mode. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: model/provider independence is not claimed; organizational independence comes from the distinct review role, separate artifact, complementary evidence surfaces, and explicit findings-to-control return path.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A - Function: sense the external scientific landscape through the literature specialist and turn those distinctions into future-facing research proposals/experiment direction consumed by later S1 work. - Disturbance / variety regulated: risk of pursuing already-solved, poorly grounded, or scientifically irrelevant directions and missing externally established methods/results that should shape the project. - Decisive decision or feedback right: select and synthesize externally retrieved papers/field distinctions that should inform the project proposal and later research program. - Decision owner: the literature Agent, with downstream proposal/experiment agents autonomously using the returned research intelligence. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: paper-search skills/web access, literature notes/memory, fixed upstream paths, shared project filesystem, and coordinator sequencing. - Closure path: literature agent searches/reads external work and writes literature notes; the proposal agent explicitly reads ../literature/notes/ and memory.md; experiment/manuscript stages consume the proposal/literature outputs, changing subsequent project work. - Boundary reachability: literature is a default first-party specialist stage in the project template, and its artifacts are wired as upstream inputs to subsequent specialists in every supported runtime. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the first-party literature Agent decides what external evidence is relevant and synthesizes it; file plumbing only returns that intelligence to current capability. - External distinction: current prior papers, methods, results, and gaps retrieved from outside the project. - Future / prospective distinction: which field gaps/constraints should shape the proposal and experiments the project has not yet executed. - Adaptation option generated: literature-grounded research/proposal directions and constraints for experiment design. - Path back into current capability / S3: persisted literature notes become explicit upstream inputs to proposal and later experiment/manuscript work under coordinator sequencing. - Evidence: folder/agent upstream-downstream architecture, research workflow skill. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: project memory and peer review alone are not used to justify S4; the credit rests on external scientific sensing and a closed return path into future project work.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no material first-party identity/ultimate-policy closure is established at the research-project recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: not established at S5 scope. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established. - Decision owner: not applicable. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: SOUL role definitions, user project brief, templates, skills, provider configuration, and needshuman escalation constrain operation but do not themselves establish ultimate-policy governance. - Closure path: no evidenced identity/policy issue → legitimate ultimate authority → authoritative decision → returned operation loop is established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the coordinator manages current work but is not shown redefining the project&#39;s ultimate identity/policy; generic human input/escalation is not automatically parent-governed S5. - Evidence: coordinator SOUL, architecture. - Basis: structural negative finding. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: a user can direct a project and the coordinator can request human input, but neither fact alone establishes S5 without a genuine ultimate-policy issue and authoritative return path.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/c9da28372ac35ff5d8bc9a74679a6987a5d451df/assessments/openags.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit c9da28372ac35ff5d8bc9a74679a6987a5d451df\">c9da283 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"4\" data-a=\"4\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-13T20:00:45+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/PawanOsman/OpenCursor\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">OpenCursor</a><small>2022</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"4\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>4</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-13\">2026-10-13</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/PawanOsman/OpenCursor/tree/b4528f95ca5ab4cddf22bef3e19431fad00feb01\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision b4528f95ca5ab4cddf22bef3e19431fad00feb01\">b4528f9 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: directly interpret, inspect, edit and verify software against a human coding task. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unknown code and runtime behavior, incomplete edits, tool errors and feedback from repository/test inspection. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose file reads, edits, tool calls, retries, delegated S1 work and final response based on live evidence. - Decision owner: the model-driven coding agent in Agent mode and coding-worker subagents in supported Project/Multitask modes. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: VS Code tool bridge, file and shell effect adapters, permission/approval policies, session persistence and job display. - Closure path: agent choice → tool execution/permission result → actual file/shell observation → subsequent model decision or answer. - Boundary reachability: the packaged extension directly wires its agent loop and repository tools, no custom embedding host or optional external agent workflow is necessary. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the acting model leaves tool wrappers but no task-driven selection/repair; human approves permitted effects but does not choose every next coding action. - Evidence: README.md; loop.ts; index.ts; files.ts; approvalPolicy.ts - Basis: explicit and structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: attenuate shared-workspace change collisions and conflicting edits between independently acting delegated code-writing workers in the shipped coordinator/team modes. - Disturbance / variety regulated: at least two concurrently active coding children may attempt incompatible modifications or overwrite/revert another worker&#39;s output in their shared checkout. - Decisive decision or feedback right: lead model selects nonoverlapping assignments, sends corrective messages when a child flags conflict, and revises affected children rather than blindly accepting conflicting edits. - Decision owner: the lead agent/model in opt-in Project/Multitask configurations; child agents report overlaps; deterministic mailbox and a concurrency cap only execute the decisions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Task schema&#39;s &#39;assign separate files&#39; contract, built-in team protocol&#39;s non-overwrite/flag instruction, stable child IDs, collaborator status, message/follow-up controls. - Closure path: child conflict signal or current roster/result → lead&#39;s scope/plan correction → SendAgentMessage/FollowupAgent or new separate assignment → children&#39;s subsequent work; separation also happens prospectively when lead initially assigns distinct files. - Boundary reachability: built-in Project/Multitask prompt paths and child/messaging tools are wired in the standard extension, not derived from a user-created orchestration script. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: task-scope choice and conflict response are discretionary lead-model decisions; the active-child cap and status store do not own that interference-specific decision. - Evidence: loop.ts; schemas.ts; teams.ts; backgroundTasks.ts; collaborationState.ts - Basis: explicit mode instructions and structural code-wired feedback - Confidence: medium; a supported decision path, not proof that every conflict is detected or prevented - Caveats: children usually share a workspace; conversation worktrees are optional isolation and not automatic per-child reservations. Ordinary Agent mode does not establish this coordinated path on every turn. - Distinct S1 units: concurrent model/tool-running coding children started via the Task tool, each with its own history, task and executable edit tools. - Inter-S1 disturbance: overwriting/reverting another child&#39;s files or incompatible edits in the shared workspace, explicitly addressed by the shipped Team protocol and Task tool. - Attenuating coordination relation: lead chooses separate file assignments; built-in team members must adapt/flag cross-member conflicts, while root can steer/interrupt/reassign them. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: child reports and roster responses enter the lead turn; message/follow-up deliver revised instructions to named still-running or resumed children. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the shipped instructions target a concrete shared-file interference, not only transporting tasks; lead feedback can alter worker scopes after conflict.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: regulate the set of active delegated commitments across current Project/Multitask coding operations. - Disturbance / variety regulated: multiple live coding children compete for bounded concurrent slots, priorities and dependencies while task results and interruptions change the feasible current plan. - Decisive decision or feedback right: inspect the complete conversation collaborator roster/status and choose which current worker gets each unit, is steered, followed up or interrupted; revise running commitments in response to results. - Decision owner: the opt-in Project/Multitask lead model; not the fixed max-four gate, job registry, event stream or human authorization engine. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ListAgents and stable IDs, activeAgents map, child status/result feedback, SendAgentMessage/FollowupAgent/InterruptAgent, root&#39;s intentionally disabled editing tools. - Closure path: whole-session roster/task reports → lead&#39;s portfolio-level assignment/intervention decision → launch/message/interrupt and changed child work → updated statuses/results. - Boundary reachability: the standard Project and Multitask modes explicitly put an LLM lead over all delegated coding tasks with model-facing operational management tools. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the lead model leaves a cap and worker status API but not a discretionary choice of how to reallocate current work; the supported lead modes are wider than one routine child tool call. - Evidence: loop.ts; schemas.ts; collaborationState.ts; backgroundTasks.ts; taskState.ts - Basis: structural and explicit first-party coordinator-mode instructions - Confidence: medium; session-scoped, not organizational or multi-deployment management - Caveats: not attributed to ordinary single-worker Agent mode or separate VS Code sessions. Does not mean that all child steps are autonomously centralized. - Whole-system current view: ListAgents exposes all retained conversation collaborators, live status/result and queued-message counts; background job registry tracks running/completed work. - Current-control decision scope: lead-mode model decides present unit assignment, bounded concurrency slot utilization, correction/interrupt, follow-up and completion of delegated commitments.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independently inspect the implementing agent&#39;s code claims using a separate read-only code-reviewer context and raw repository/diff evidence, then return findings for control of repairs. - Disturbance / variety regulated: an implementer&#39;s self-report or ordinary integrated tool-status check may omit real defects, concurrency problems or unsafe edits. - Decisive decision or feedback right: a separate reviewer agent evaluates the actual diff and source context for defects; the implementing parent receives and decides follow-up repair or rejection. - Decision owner: the delegated reviewer model for the complementary judgment, acting only when commissioned, and the lead/implementer model for the downstream corrective choice. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: built-in read-only code-reviewer and team-code-reviewer roles, child independent history, file-reading/search tools, returned report and parent Task result. - Closure path: parent requests review → separate child reads current repository/diff and forms defect-first findings → report returns to parent history → implementing lead can fix, stop or ask for further check. - Boundary reachability: built-in code-reviewer Task type and built-in team-code-reviewer are available in the shipped extension and run through first-party child-model/tool execution; no user-authored reviewer is needed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the reviewer performs an independent direct-evidence judgment; parent runtime reports/forwards but does not supply the reviewer&#39;s verdict. Ordinary VerificationLedger is excluded from S3 credit. - Evidence: teams.ts; schemas.ts; loop.ts; files.ts; verification.ts - Basis: explicit built-in independent reviewer instructions and source-wired child execution - Confidence: medium-high for opt-in separate-context code review, not for default every-turn checks - Caveats: review and corrective response are conditional on the model/parent invoking the supported reviewer; no claim of Organization S3 governance or fully independent remote operator. - Claim being audited: whether implementing-agent code modifications are correct, safe and maintainable. - Ordinary reporting path: implementer tool outputs, final answer and VerificationLedger test-status summary. - Complementary access path: read-only separately instantiated reviewer model with direct code/diff/repository access and explicit defect-first prompt. - Independence boundary: different child model context/history/role and direct artifacts rather than merely parsing the implementer&#39;s natural-language self-report. - Who acts on findings: calling lead/implementer receives findings through Task result and can select corrective work.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no separate external-and-prospective organizational capability-adaptation loop is established in the distributed extension. - Disturbance / variety regulated: external documentation, model/tool changes and potential future feature requirements. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no shipped S4-specific adaptive decision/feedback owner identified. - Decision owner: model for present coding and documentation retrieval; user or extension settings for selecting models/skills; neither closes future adaptation of the harness organization. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: docs discovery/indexing, semantic repository retrieval, reusable personas/instructions, task memory and provider choice. - Closure path: information returns to task context, but no first-party future capability strategy is selected and returned to ongoing operational capability. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: document discovery and planning for current coding work is not a prospective S4 organizational judgment. - Evidence: docsPlanner.ts; loop.ts; README.md; taskState.ts - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no identity/ultimate-policy constitutional decision path with binding return is supplied for the coding-session organization. - Disturbance / variety regulated: ultimate purpose or policy conflicts as distinct from one tool&#39;s allowed/denied action. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no first-party S5 decisive authority; ordinary permission review authorizes individual actions. - Decision owner: user/host settings and project operators outside the assessed system-in-focus; no evidenced internal/parent constitutional decision chain. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: approval policy, read-only modes, tool restrictions, workspace instructions and team role prompts. - Closure path: action approvals return allow/deny to ordinary task execution; no identity-level matter → parent ultimate decision → changed governing operation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: tool policy is a safety constraint, not the identity-policy function; an operator&#39;s wider development organization is not imported into this one-session boundary. - Evidence: approvalPolicy.ts; loop.ts; schemas.ts; README.md; teams.ts - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/37c1aace4e11d50dbdda13b256534152d0169eea/assessments/opencursor.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 37c1aace4e11d50dbdda13b256534152d0169eea\">37c1aac &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-03T17:43:47+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/DarkDvr/Synapse\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Synapse</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-03\">2026-11-03</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/DarkDvr/Synapse/tree/b91345750a7d8ec1fca1557cdf33613762794223\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision b91345750a7d8ec1fca1557cdf33613762794223\">b913457 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Autonomous local production and checking of a specific delegated project deliverable. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Ambiguous requirements, codebase/tool errors, rendered defects and incomplete subtasks encountered during task execution. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Role model chooses its own next implementation/test tool after observing results. - Decision owner: Role-specific model agent, reached through Synapse-created engine session; human owns separate scope/permission gates. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: OpenCode adapter, per-role tool rules, session brief, tool transcript, provider retries, bounded worker runner and written-artifact ledger. - Closure path: Model tool call→real engine execution→tool feedback→continuing role turn→worker report/files, then return to Architect. - Boundary reachability: Shipped Multi mode makes genuine model-backed role sessions rather than invoking a development mock. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The role&#39;s model has task-sensitive tool/repair choice; Synapse supplies the enclosing execution and evidence channel. - Evidence: OpenCode adapter, runs.js, plans.js. - Basis: structural - Confidence: high on reachable code path; no independent Windows-provider execution. - Caveats: Actual model quality, installed/packaged engine availability and end-to-end completion were not live-tested.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: Feedback-based regulation of interference or oscillation among operational S1 task agents. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Concurrent edits, shared project plan corruption and worker cross-role overwrite risks. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No specific agent-owned inter-worker collision detection→mediation→returned adjustment evidenced. - Decision owner: Deterministic harness serialization and folder rules; Architect decides task sequence as S3 commitment, not evidenced as S2 conflict mediation. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: One-active-worker busy guard per project, roleWriteRules, plan ownership, role folders and assignment ladder. - Closure path: Serial admission and static write rules prevent some conflicts but do not inspect actual inter-unit interference and negotiate mutual change. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Transport, isolation and top-down task allocation alone are insufficient for S2 at this recursion. - Evidence: runs.js#L546, plans.js. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium-high for the reviewed first-party orchestration paths. - Caveats: A model could discuss competing tasks in an individual session, but no reusable S2-specific interference closure was established.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Whole-system current view: Architect reads the project-wide plan.json task statuses, blocked items, confirmed features and per-worker completion reports grounded in runs.jsonl and actual file/tool records. - Current-control decision scope: Architect alone accepts/rejects task completion, alters the whole plan and selects corrective work and next role commitments within approved project scope; a runner&#39;s retry/backoff merely enforces constraints. - Function: Current project-wide commitment, progress and corrective task regulation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Partial deliverables, faulty worker claims, blocked tasks, failed providers, scope drift and new defects threatening the approved project goal. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Judge each reported worker outcome, update the plan, mark done/retry/blocked, reprioritize or delegate the next current task. - Decision owner: Model-driven Architect in Multi mode, constrained by operator-approved features and optional automatic continuation. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: plan.json as Architect-only authority, actual runs.jsonl, injected post-worker report, task-role assignment, retry fallback, progress/status rail. - Closure path: Worker run evidence→synthetic report→Architect evaluation/update of plan→next delegate or required operator escalation; Keep building enables continuation, while off returns after each judged task. - Boundary reachability: delegate MCP, run ledger, handleTurn, report and wake are connected in shipped Multi mode. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Removing the Architect leaves recorded runs and deterministic stops but not discretionary whole-project acceptance, replanning or corrective commitment. - Evidence: runs.js#L2741, plans.js#L1270, delegate MCP. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium-high; project-control closure is code-visible, not a live success claim. - Caveats: An operator can pause or steer a task, but the documented toggles do not independently establish a distinct parent-governed S3 decision mode.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Claim being audited: Implementing worker&#39;s claim that the shipped build is usable, faithful to approved design and free of target regressions. - Ordinary reporting path: Coder sends its own transcript, modified-file list and completion statement to Architect through runs.jsonl and the synthetic result message. - Complementary access path: Separate QC model role uses the delivered application, renders/screenshots UI, diffs approved mock, tests unusual inputs and writes reproducible findings with IDs independently of worker prose. - Independence boundary: QC has its own configurable model/session and role folder; it cannot edit the build and coders cannot write the QC findings folder under Synapse&#39;s first-party role rules. This is role separation, not tamper-proof institutional independence. - Who acts on findings: Architect reads QC output and delegates the numbered fix to a coder, then assigns verification of that exact repro to QC again, up to the three-round cap. - Function: Independent role-level challenge to implementation claims using real build behavior and a returned quality verdict. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Worker reports that conceal unusable UI, clipping, misleading states, poor validation or regressions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: QC chooses and executes adversarial/use-oriented checks, records reproducible findings and re-verifies fixes separately from implementing coders. - Decision owner: Dedicated configured qc model/session, distinct from coder role, with its own findings area and read-only stance toward the build. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: QC mandate to render/use the built artifact, screenshot/mock diffs, finding IDs and repros, separate folder permissions, bounded three-round QC/fix/verification plan. - Closure path: QC direct inspection→qcfindings/qc-N.md with evidence→Architect receives report and assigns specific fix task→QC re-runs repros and verifies correction. - Boundary reachability: First-party QC role, delegated real engine session, report loop and role-write restrictions are shipped in Multi mode. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: QC independently determines and reports actual product defects; the Architect, not QC, retains current-control commitment changes. - Evidence: plans.js#L700, plans.js#L1342, runs.js#L1002. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium: independently tasked source-visible QC path, with no performed cross-provider execution or efficacy audit. - Caveats: The product does not establish strong organizational or cryptographic isolation; different role/model configuration and artifact-based repro constitute the limited claimed independence. If QC is never delegated in a particular plan, the optional path is inactive.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-unknown\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: ? - Function: Sensing external/future needs and developing options returned to current project capability. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unanticipated user needs and future features for the product under construction. - Decisive decision or feedback right: An Inventor model proposes and ranks extra features; operator chooses scope and Architect links confirmed features to tasks. Whether this amounts to an external sensing→adaptation→S3 loop is unresolved. - Decision owner: Inventor generates options and operator accepts features; no proved future-environment evidence trail connecting the two. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Inventor role mandate, optional local search tools, ideas file, confirmed/rejected features.json and Architect planning instructions. - Closure path: Proposed feature→human selection→confirmed feature/task is explicit; source does not establish that the proposal derived from an observed external prospective distinction rather than imagined additions to a current wish. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Generating project feature ideas is not, alone, a demonstrated VSM S4 process; the active inventor path prevents a defensible categorical absence claim. - Evidence: plans.js#L660, plans.js#L1287, README. - Basis: structural - Confidence: bounded uncertainty. - Caveats: Direct evidence of actual external future-condition observation and its governing choice would resolve the state; neither was reproduced at this source pin.\">?</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: Legitimate ultimate-policy or system identity dispute resolution at project recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Conflicts over project identity, ultimate purpose and governing policy rather than current task scope. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No first-party identity-level issue→authority decision→returned governing policy path shown. - Decision owner: Human operator chooses product features, permission and continuity toggles; those remain operational/requirement decisions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Feature selection forms, prompt constraints, role-specific permission rules and operator approval. - Closure path: Scope selection reaches project plan, but no recognized ultimate-policy decision channel governs a dispute about the system&#39;s identity. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Choosing which mock to build or whether to continue is not an S5 legitimacy mechanism. - Evidence: plans.js#L1287, automation.js, permissions.js. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: External real-world product governance or developer organizational decisions are out of the assessed standard-distribution boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/7211b7addb042e94e57c4d3d7934a31e2d314368/assessments/da/darkdvr-synapse.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 7211b7addb042e94e57c4d3d7934a31e2d314368\">7211b7a &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-01T16:05:09+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/MattGrundy/leadkit\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Leadkit</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-01\">2026-11-01</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/MattGrundy/leadkit/tree/3efcde576a5f687b0013fad902fe9cff8be11766\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 3efcde576a5f687b0013fad902fe9cff8be11766\">3efcde5 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: implement project-scoped coding and verification tasks through agent-selected reading, editing, testing and tool use. - Disturbance / variety regulated: variable code requests, test outcomes, repository content and task-specific errors. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the installed developer/specialist Claude Code Agent decides how to perform its assigned task and reacts to tool observations and reviewer feedback. - Decision owner: active model-driven role Agent in Claude Code Teams, configured by Leadkit&#39;s shipped first-party role definitions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Claude Code model/tool host, installed role allowlist, task files, path guard, queue/status verbs and project profile. - Closure path: tools return output to the active agent; it changes/tests code, records evidence and returns its result to task/reviewer queue. - Boundary reachability: standard adoption installs the role definitions and wires hooks; the deployed Host Agent Teams directly consumes these, whereas bare leadkit CLI-only execution is explicitly not claimed to be autonomous. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the role agent chooses substantive code edits and validation steps; CLI/guards enforce boundaries and do not generate the operational choices. - Evidence: internal/starter/agents/developer.md#L1-L40; internal/starter/agents/lead.md#L1-L42; internal/starter/starter.go#L1-L85; docs/ADOPTING-LEADKIT.md#L1-L105. - Basis: explicit / structural within adopted supported mode. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: model execution belongs to external Claude Code; no live paid Claude Code Teams E2E was performed, and disabled Teams yields no autonomous team.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Distinct S1 units: lead-assigned developer and specialist Agents working potentially overlapping git paths and successive reviews, each with its own task/owner identity. - Inter-S1 disturbance: concurrent edits to the same path can silently undo peer work; premature parallel critique can review a SHA invalidated by prior code-review fixes; orphaned task ownership can stall delivery. - Attenuating coordination relation: lead Agent deliberately assigns path ownership, sequences the code-review and critique sessions, returns failures to original owner, and orchestrator Agent resolves stale/idle queue handoffs. Path/queue and gate checks enforce these decisions. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: reviewer findings change the owner&#39;s next implementation, a changed commit invalidates old approval, and queue/orphan status causes reassignment or escalation rather than duplicate work. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: these mechanisms target concrete shared-path collision, stale-SHA review race and lost-ownership hazards and return corrective outcomes to specific work units. - Function: attenuate interference among parallel project Agents so ownership and review outcomes remain consistent. - Disturbance / variety regulated: shared-file contention, role drift, stale review race, lost work and duplicate ownership. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Lead chooses paths and reviewer order; the orchestrator chooses how to handle queue stalls within documented roles; deterministic planner provides constraints and signals. - Decision owner: model-driven Lead/Orchestrator Agents in the shipped enabled-team operating mode, not the Go planner or filesystem alone. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ownership guard, role lanes, task IDs, deterministic plan, reviewer chain SHA binding and distinct identities. - Closure path: owner assignment enters task store/queue, agents act within scoped paths, review findings return to builder, and next verified SHA advances review/ship stages. - Boundary reachability: these roles and paths are installed through the public adoption workflow and use active Claude Code Teams Agent/SendMessage capabilities, not only project maintainer dogfood. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the meaningful assignment and remediation selection belongs to autonomous role Agents; guards deny inconsistent actions but do not choose the route. - Evidence: internal/starter/agents/lead.md#L1-L42; internal/starter/agents/orchestrator.md#L1-L28; internal/starter/team-protocol.md#L1-L100; internal/orchestrate/plan.go#L1-L130; internal/gate/chain.go#L65-L140. - Basis: explicit / structural. - Confidence: medium-high for standard deployed role protocol. - Caveats: coordination relies on following first-party role instructions; the Go CLI does not itself launch or monitor a proprietary model session.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: C(P) - Whole-system current view: project queue/status/owner/priority/dependency records plus live roster passed in from Agent Teams; account burn telemetry, workstream count, budget reserve and pause-lock status. - Current-control decision scope: choose active team workload and concurrency, defer dispatch when budget is stressed, prioritize critical tasks, requeue orphaned work or grant owner-directed staffing/priority changes. - Function: maintain present project-team commitments, workload and throughput within user resource and authority bounds. - Disturbance / variety regulated: misallocated role capacity, budget burn, stuck tasks and competing current priorities. - Decisive decision or feedback right: first-party planner exposes S3-specific route/requeue/hold/triage choices, while the installed autonomous actor must assemble a live roster and act on higher-level capacity/priority reports; in the parent mode the human selects the active roster/priority/parallelism and authorizes changes. - Decision owner: C constructor has deterministic CLI and Agent-facing role protocol but lacks a self-contained autonomous S3 governor with all decisive rights; P mode is the legitimate project operator who decides and applies current resource/priority constraints. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: leadkit status, burn/priority planner, usage history, account pause lock, role roster, task queue, apply confirmation, waker/sweep daemons. - Closure path: computed route/hold/escalation is returned to orchestrator or human; accepted priority/roster/budget decisions alter task status or available consumers, and subsequent queue/burn samples expose the result. - Boundary reachability: CLI+roles+hooks/daemons are standard installed interfaces, including explicit owner changes, not private governance; C still needs external Agent Teams and operator adoption, P needs actual human choice. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: consuming a deterministic burn verdict and routing plan does not by itself make the autonomous Lead own a whole-system capacity tradeoff. Parent control is a distinct explicit operating mode rather than a decoration on an approval button. - Evidence: internal/orchestrate/plan.go#L1-L130; internal/orchestrate/apply.go#L1-L80; cmd/leadkit/cmdorchestrate.go#L1-L110; internal/burn/burn.go#L1-L115; internal/burn/throttle.go#L1-L70; docs/ADOPTING-LEADKIT.md#L20-L105. - Basis: explicit / structural. - Confidence: medium; project-specific actual authorization and agent roster are deployment-specific. - Caveats: automatic pause/resume is deterministic enforcement; the C notation is restricted to S3-specific live-resource decision paths, not arbitrary queue commands. Parent mode presumes a real operator uses reports to revise priority/roster rather than merely installing a static config.\">C(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Claim being audited: a code diff at a pinned SHA is correct/safe and actually serves the ticket&#39;s intended goal. - Ordinary reporting path: builder Agent&#39;s completion claim, code changes and test logs. - Complementary access path: separate read-only code-review and critique Agents inspect diff, task and surrounding code under different questions and record own SHA-bound verdicts/evidence. - Independence boundary: distinct installed roles/tools (reviewers lack Edit/Write/SendMessage), order and different-role gate checks; reviewers are not the builder and may not file the other layer&#39;s verdict. - Who acts on findings: original builder/lead receives changes-needed; gate blocks push/PR path until matching independent ready layers, then finalise/human may proceed. - Function: complementary audit of operational output and purpose before a consequential code shipment. - Disturbance / variety regulated: self-certified implementation errors, wrong goal, stale-SHA approval, and review-role collusion/impersonation accidents. - Decisive decision or feedback right: separate model-driven reviewer Agents make substantive correctness and goal-fit judgments, each recorded on observed commit evidence. - Decision owner: reviewer Agents own their respective judgments, while Go gate and forge enforcement decide only whether required current, role-separated records exist. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: reviewer read-only grants, mutation-evidence notes, chain/order, SHA stamps, status/queue, push hooks and optional verified server protection. - Closure path: changes-needed returns work to developer, revised SHA invalidates old approvals, both ready verdicts permit finalise/push within granted authority. - Boundary reachability: reviewer roles and gate are shipped/installable first-party components in the normal team setup, not merely CI tests; the external Claude Code host executes reviewers. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: semantic findings belong to independent review Agents; checking SHA/order/identity and command gating is deterministic enforcement. - Evidence: internal/starter/agents/code-review-agent.md#L1-L65; internal/starter/agents/critique-agent.md#L1-L85; internal/gate/chain.go#L1-L140; internal/starter/team-protocol.md#L35-L100; cmd/leadkit/cmdgate.go#L1-L95. - Basis: explicit / structural. - Confidence: medium-high for first-party role separation and gate. - Caveats: recorded --by identity may be self-asserted when host does not bind CLAUDEAGENTNAME; local hooks are bypassable without forge protection. Multiple reviewers may share provider/model family; independence is role/evidence separation, not externally certified audit.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no evidenced external-future sensing, adaptation-option judgment and return into current project capability at the assessed boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: emerging technology/organizational threats or future opportunities are not a first-party recurrent object of control. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no first-party S4 actor chooses strategic redesign from external signals and deploys it. - Decision owner: not established; humans may modify roles or open issues; normal Lead coordination operates on present tasks. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: feedback-as-issues in overarching-lead protocol, role capture, postmortems, repo governance and backlog. - Closure path: issues preserve potential improvements but no closed strategic adoption loop into team capabilities is wired. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: planning future tasks and capturing lessons is not necessarily outside-and-then adaptation. - Evidence: internal/starter/agents/overarching-lead.md#L1-L110; internal/starter/starter.go#L1-L100; README.md#L75-L145. - Basis: structural bounded absence. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: the user can commission tool upgrades; they remain discretionary external work, not a shipped S4 mode. ### Absence scope\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: P - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: whether a project team may grant itself standing rights to push, create PRs, merge or modify protected repository policy, versus retaining those fundamental authorization rights at the repository owner. - Ultimate authority in each claimed mode: direct project/repository owner alone; peer lead and overarching coordinator cannot grant or relay authority in lieu of the owner. - Return-to-operation path: owner-approved authority.&lt;repo&gt; entry in the project profile changes guards and governed PR/push commands; protected-branch rules can be installed and read back, and subsequent operations are permitted/blocked by the returned grant. - Function: reserve ultimate scope of consequential agent action to direct human owner authority and apply it as a durable team constitution. - Disturbance / variety regulated: agent self-grant, laundering authority through a peer coordinator, unreviewed merges/pushes and inconsistent rights across repositories. - Decisive decision or feedback right: grant or deny standing repository mutation/merge authority, and amend protected-branch policy under owner mandate. - Decision owner: legitimate human project/repository owner in the shipped parent-governed setup, not the autonomous Lead or other session. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: per-repo authority profile, adoption stop point, PR/push guard, default-deny allowlists, forge protection verification and doctor. - Closure path: team must request direct owner grant; owner decision is written to profile/forge policy; subsequent hook/CLI operations consume it, permit only granted action and report denials. Denial leaves default-deny intact. - Boundary reachability: authority-grant step and policy guards are part of normal adoption flow and CLI; code explicitly reads named per-repo authority, so the mode is real without relying on GitHub contributors as a metasystem. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: agents cannot legitimately establish their own standing constitutional authority merely by relaying a request; the owner remains the sole source of grants, while Go enforces the returned decision. - Evidence: docs/ADOPTING-LEADKIT.md#L20-L105; internal/starter/agents/overarching-lead.md#L1-L110; README.md#L75-L145; docs/design/agent-sandboxing-and-enforcement.md#L1-L95; cmd/leadkit/cmdgate.go#L1-L95. - Basis: explicit / structural parent-governed closure. - Confidence: medium-high for first-party policy path, not for an unseen specific live owner decision. - Caveats: ordinary per-PR human review is not counted as S5; standing authority is the constitutional object. Client-side hooks alone are bypassable; host/forge enforcement and real owner-supplied profile must be configured.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/a4ff63c7225d233941c9c33cd63d66584ef61ae4/assessments/le/leadkit.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit a4ff63c7225d233941c9c33cd63d66584ef61ae4\">a4ff63c &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-02T18:09:58+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/ebetica/recurse\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Recurse</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-02\">2026-11-02</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/ebetica/recurse/tree/6e4d231e299a14f19fe6eee9d9f7f43781780c2a\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 6e4d231e299a14f19fe6eee9d9f7f43781780c2a\">6e4d231 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: produce code/repository outcomes through independently model-directed persistent work on a task, including observation, edits, tests, terminal commands and iteration. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing code state, command/test failures, incomplete context, long-running commands and interruptions/restarts. - Decisive decision or feedback right: each agent chooses its subsequent tools, edits, tests, recovery, further child tasks or final result from observed feedback. - Decision owner: the Pi Durable model actor in each root/child conversation, subject to external operator authorization and safety-tool constraints. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: per-agent AgentNode, Durable Harness, background shell/wait/monitor, persisted messages, working-directory state and safety checks. - Closure path: operator/parent task → model-selected tools/edits → shell/file/agent observations and completion wakes → subsequent model decision and result report; durable work can resume across browser disconnection and restart. - Boundary reachability: normal recurse /path starts a real agent; documented subagent create starts more independent model agents from CLI without a special development fixture. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model turns choose task-level actions and responses; deterministic process supervision only transports results and gates execution. - Evidence: quickstart and purpose, runtime guide, Durable turns. - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Distinct S1 units: two or more independently running implementer child agents with separate model turns, local tasks and repository-editing decisions, under one parent tree. - Inter-S1 disturbance: simultaneous implementation in one checkout can overwrite, interleave or destabilize another child&#39;s in-progress file changes; the official guide explicitly motivates separate worktrees to prevent this shared-write interference. - Attenuating coordination relation: the parent model assigns separate named managed Git worktrees during subagent create --worktree, so concurrent implementers operate against isolated working files and branches; parent/worker exchange and eventual review/merge reconcile outcomes rather than expose the same working tree to competing writers. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: the chosen worktree path becomes the spawned child&#39;s effective working directory for its later tool calls; its branch or PR comes back as an integration artifact, and the parent can message/reassign a child or defer/alter a merge when work overlaps. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the intervention targets a named code-edit collision between distinct S1 producers; it changes their actual workspace topology and future write targets. Messaging alone, ordinary task splitting or generic queueing is not credited. - Function: damp parallel implementation interference by deciding and maintaining task-specific checkout separation and integrating branch-level results. - Disturbance / variety regulated: concurrent editing races and inadvertent shared-workspace corruption; downstream logical Git merge conflicts remain possible. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose which child receives a fresh checkout and task/branch, and adjust work allocation or subsequent merge handling after reports. - Decision owner: parent model-led agent when operating the documented parallel-worktree mode; deterministic Git/worktree holder machinery implements the isolation decision. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: subagent.ts --worktree, worktree.ts named checkouts and holder claims, per-agent cwd, Git branches, child messages/final reports. - Closure path: parent recognizes parallel edits → assigns separate worktrees → each child modifies its own checkout → branch/PR or feedback returns → parent selects review, integration, clarification or further task allocation; future child work remains separated. - Boundary reachability: the shipped CLI and runtime support worktree-bearing spawns; the first-party guide explicitly recommends and illustrates this normal multi-agent delivery pattern, not an unimplemented suggestion. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the parent model elects when/how to separate work and which tasks/branches to assign; static worktree creation, holder bookkeeping and Git merge mechanics are enforcement, not autonomous coordinators. - Evidence: worktrees, multiagent and collision purpose, child creation interface, managed checkouts and claims, example agent graph. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium - Caveats: there is no automatic semantic merge-conflict arbitrator; S2 here is model-selected preventive checkout isolation and returned branch handling, not guaranteed conflict-free integration. One-agent or shared-cwd use does not exercise it.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Whole-system current view: the parent&#39;s model-request snapshot lists its child/descendant status and queued inputs, while native subagent reads/waits/messages expose individual work and results; the supervisor maintains authoritative live membership and lifecycle status. - Current-control decision scope: the parent can choose/constrain concurrent commitments and effort on creation, interrupt or redirect active children, reassign with move, retire stale work cells and integrate or rework their output as the joint goal demands. - Function: govern a coding tree&#39;s ongoing resource/commitment allocation and exceptional intervention while independent implementers own their local tasks. - Disturbance / variety regulated: stalled agents, poor distribution of effort, late user requirements, active conflicts or incomplete child tasks and divergent branch outcomes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose whether to launch, interrupt, redirect, reparent or retire child work and whether its returned work satisfies the whole task. - Decision owner: model-backed parent agent in the supported multiagent tree; operator may intervene or take over, but operator interaction is not assumed to constitute an independent whole-team S3 parent mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: live Supervisor/tree projection, agent digest, subagent.ts create/send/interrupt/wait/move/rm, Durable report holds and completion delivery, fixed resource/process limits. - Closure path: status/child output/monitor/user steer → parent sees current tree information → parent selects task or resource intervention → supervisor/CLI changes relevant child agent and sends new input → changed work and updated status return to parent. - Boundary reachability: the agent prompt includes a live tree digest; the shipped CLI supports the control operations under normal local execution, and child reports reach the parent automatically. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the parent model decides interventions and current tradeoffs; the supervisor enforces ownership/fencing and routes commands but is not itself a discretionary S3 agent. Merely having a class named Supervisor is insufficient. - Evidence: parent model tree digest, CLI control surface, tree ownership and interventions, child report closure. - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no first-party standard operating audit process is evidenced that independently inspects completed operational results and returns findings to current control. - Disturbance / variety regulated: optimistic code-completion claims, faulty edits or unsafe commands could require independent checking. - Decisive decision or feedback right: inspect raw delivered artifacts outside ordinary child reporting and issue a complementary verdict with corrective return; no installed closed path established. - Decision owner: pi-safety uses a separate model to grant/deny proposed actions; an optional reviewer child can be authored by a user, but there is no default complementary outcome auditor. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: separate model safety reviewer, action transcript, built-in denial gate, recipe for a review-agent graph, source tests and developer review rules. - Closure path: safety&#39;s pre-execution DENY returns a reason and can change the next actor proposal; this regulates permissions, not independent observation of the action&#39;s real outcome. User-authored review scripts may create further loops but are not established here as an active standard process. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the second model genuinely judges proposed tool authorization, but its evidence deliberately excludes tool outputs, edited file content and post-execution result; its separate model identity does not make it complementary operational audit. - Evidence: review evidence boundary and fail-open behavior, user-composed reviewer example, agent report path. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no distinct prospective environment-model and capability adaptation loop is established beyond ordinary coding tasks and event-driven continuation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing provider/tool ecosystems, CI trends, new project requirements and future architectural possibilities. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose a new whole-harness capability based on external/future distinctions and carry it back into live operation; not evidenced. - Decision owner: agents can investigate and change repository code when tasked, while users/developers configure tools and skills; no autonomous organization-level S4 owner is shipped. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: background monitors, memory, MCP configuration, skills, models and task-local planning. - Closure path: a CI/deployment monitor can wake an agent for immediate handling, but a signal-to-task reaction is not itself prospective option formation and a capability-adaptation return to S3. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: planning future code steps is S1 task choice; Recurse&#39;s extensibility offers means to construct S4 but no first-party defined future/environment adaptation decision loop. - Evidence: monitor and goals examples, extensibility principles, runtime guide. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no distinct runtime identity/ultimate-policy adjudicator is established for the multiagent coding organization. - Disturbance / variety regulated: risky commands, operator intent conflicts, policy exceptions, agent takeovers and security-mode choices. - Decisive decision or feedback right: settle a whole-organization identity or ultimate-policy tension and return a binding new rule; no closed function-specific path shown. - Decision owner: operator/developer owns Recurse permissions and safety instructions; model safety reviewer applies predefined action policy, and child/parent agents follow ordinary delegated task authority. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: user task prompts, first-party pi-safety and custom additive policy rules, operator-only takeover, tool denial circuit breaker and user consent for exact actions. - Closure path: model reviewer may return DENY and request a safer action or explicit authorization; that decides an individual command under existing policy, not a new identity-governing rule for the organization. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a model can reason about dangerous operations, but ultimate authority to change policy is not delegated to a live S5 body with a policy-level issue/escalation and operational return. - Evidence: safety review limits, operator takeover, takeover state/authority. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/e94effaef30205f31fb915634491cbab971ca89d/assessments/re/recurse.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit e94effaef30205f31fb915634491cbab971ca89d\">e94effa &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-02T16:47:33+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/BubbleBuffer/BubblePaw-Core\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">BubblePaw Core</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-02\">2026-11-02</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/BubbleBuffer/BubblePaw-Core/tree/2f98827beda9f2c988368c99ee9fa69120899dff\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 2f98827beda9f2c988368c99ee9fa69120899dff\">2f98827 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Perform workspace tasks through durable model/tool sessions and reusable child work actors. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Uncertain task progress, tool errors, context limits, interrupted external effects and child questions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose next action, tool, child task or final answer from the current observations. - Decision owner: The model actor driven by the native Harness model/tool loop. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: SQLite history/effects, ToolDispatcher, CodeMode/workspace tools, provider adapters, approvals, queued steering and explicit resume. - Closure path: Prepared model request → model-selected tools → durable tool results → subsequent model request; child completion can wake the parent. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Own runtime.rs executes the authoritative tool loop. The API provider witnesses inference-only transport; external Codex/Claude adapters do not donate their inner control functions. - Evidence: crates/bubblepaw-core/src/runtime.rs; crates/bubblepaw-core/src/dispatch.rs; crates/bubblepaw-runner/src/agents.rs; docs/providers.md; docs/child-sessions.md. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: Source-level assessment; no live model/provider execution is claimed. - Boundary reachability: The normal bubblepaw runner wires Harness, tools and model providers; API provider mode and registered child profiles reach the same first-party loop.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: Attenuate shared-workspace write interference between autonomous workspace child sessions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Two workspace-profile children otherwise inherit the same filesystem and can overwrite one another; descendants may accidentally use the wrong root workspace. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose isolated child workspaces for concurrent writers and use returned branch/workspace state when continuing them. - Decision owner: The parent model actor selects worktree:true through the shipped tools.agent interface; git and workspace resolvers enforce that choice. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Per-child git worktree/branch, nearest-isolated-ancestor workspace resolution, scope-bound file/exec tools, status receipts and safe retention/cleanup. - Closure path: Parent selects isolation → child runs against its effective checkout → spawn/status returns workspace/branch/worktree facts → parent continues/steers the corresponding session without sharing its write surface. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The credited decision is the agent-selectable conflict-attenuation mode, not deterministic git or session plurality. It is available to the running parent without custom host coordination code. - Evidence: docs/child-worktrees.md; docs/child-sessions.md; crates/bubblepaw-runner/src/agents.rs; crates/bubblepaw-runner/src/worktrees.rs. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: Optional mode; default workspaces are shared, and isolation does not automatically merge conflicting results or prove semantic conflict resolution.. - Boundary reachability: Registered model-facing workspace profiles expose the worktree field; agents.rs creates the binding at admission and subsequent child tools resolve that same workspace.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: Regulate current commitments and compute across a parent task and its descendant work tree. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Stale/running/failed/waiting children, finite shared tool allowance/deadline and unfinished commitments competing for that allowance. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Inspect descendant status, cancel or steer child commitments, and assign fresh per-child tool/deadline bounds for the next turn. - Decision owner: The parent model actor owns these current choices within host-imposed maximums. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Descendant-tree status with unread/results, stale-child reminders, child completion wake, inherited budget/deadline clamping and subtree cancellation cleanup. - Closure path: State/reminder → parent observes current descendant tree/result → parent cancels, redirects or issues a bounded next turn → child runtime applies the changed commitment/allowance and returns status/results. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Credit is bounded to the parent task organization: the actor sees and can change ongoing child work on behalf of that whole. Host quota waiting and concurrency limits merely enforce ceilings; they do not own the parent decision. - Evidence: docs/child-sessions.md; crates/bubblepaw-runner/src/agents.rs; crates/bubblepaw-runner/src/agenthandles.rs; crates/bubblepaw-core/src/runtime.rs. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: Spawn/decomposition alone is not the witness. Controls cannot enlarge an active turn by steering; fresh limits apply on later child turns and remain clamped by originating bounds. No separate parent-human mode is inferred from approval UI.. - Boundary reachability: The ordinary runner model tool set includes agentstatus, agentsend and cancel with the originating parent ownership checks; no custom supervisor is needed for these decisions.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: No complementary independent operational-audit loop is supplied in the normal workspace runner. - Disturbance / variety regulated: The possible organizational disturbance associated with the inspected mechanisms. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No function-specific decisive organizational right established in this supported mode. - Decision owner: No qualifying owner established; developer/operator constraints remain external decisions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Context-recall probes/judges, replay/chapter-bench commands, direct child transcript access, logs, structured-output validation and ordinary tool checks. - Closure path: No corresponding organizational return loop established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Probe scoring belongs to explicit replay/evaluation rather than a normal-session corrective controller. Generic research/child tools can be composed into review, but no specifically wired independent audit judgment plus corrective return is shipped for normal workspace operations. Source-level verification is adjacent. - Evidence: crates/bubblepaw-runner/src/probes.rs; crates/bubblepaw-runner/src/main.rs; crates/bubblepaw-core/src/observation.rs; docs/verification.md; docs/child-sessions.md. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: Source-level assessment; no live model/provider execution is claimed.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No outside-and-then organizational adaptation loop is supplied. - Disturbance / variety regulated: The possible organizational disturbance associated with the inspected mechanisms. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No function-specific decisive organizational right established in this supported mode. - Decision owner: No qualifying owner established; developer/operator constraints remain external decisions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Summary/chapter decision models, compaction/recall, selectable model roles, live provider configuration and research children. - Closure path: No corresponding organizational return loop established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Context organization and provider reconfiguration preserve current operation. Research and recall mechanisms do not by themselves investigate prospective environmental change and return selected capability adaptations. Replay model comparisons are evaluation, not a deployed adaptation authority. - Evidence: docs/decision-providers.md; docs/live-config.md; docs/context-compaction.md; crates/bubblepaw-runner/src/agents.rs; crates/bubblepaw-runner/src/probes.rs. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: Source-level assessment; no live model/provider execution is claimed.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No identity/ultimate-policy arbitration loop is supplied. - Disturbance / variety regulated: The possible organizational disturbance associated with the inspected mechanisms. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No function-specific decisive organizational right established in this supported mode. - Decision owner: No qualifying owner established; developer/operator constraints remain external decisions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Ask/Allow/Deny/Auto approvals, model-based bounded auto checks, profile narrowing, live config updates and inherited child authority. - Closure path: No corresponding organizational return loop established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The returned decisions concern ordinary tool permissions and execution constraints, not an identity/ultimate-policy matter at this recursion. No first-party escalation and authoritative arbitration/return path for that function is packaged. - Evidence: docs/approvals.md; docs/live-config.md; crates/bubblepaw-core/src/approvals/mod.rs; crates/bubblepaw-core/src/approvals/modes.rs; docs/child-sessions.md. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: Source-level assessment; no live model/provider execution is claimed.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/9ff383b7ec14a8c25bdb4ee0ad185837493720aa/assessments/bu/bubblepaw-core.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 9ff383b7ec14a8c25bdb4ee0ad185837493720aa\">9ff383b &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-01T15:34:05+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/NeoPanic/harness3\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Harness³</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-01\">2026-11-01</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/NeoPanic/harness3/tree/cb6c8e57eb0adac3f2ec1f4da1a98e52e2967063\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision cb6c8e57eb0adac3f2ec1f4da1a98e52e2967063\">cb6c8e5 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: execute bounded user-published work through model-selected tools, report results, and optionally decide to split tasks for peer execution. - Disturbance / variety regulated: variable tasks, tool results, failed actions and results from completed children. - Decisive decision or feedback right: an instantiated Agent chooses next model/tool action, whether to use the board claim/split tools, and its work contribution. - Decision owner: model-driven independent Agent within the shipped Harness run; platform validates tool and task authority but does not choose the Agent&#39;s tool sequence. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Preset bindings, runtime policy, receipts/checkpoints, SQLite Run state, tool-safety limits and claim prerequisite. - Closure path: executed tool output feeds subsequent model reasoning; settled Run and child results feed board continuation or user-visible contribution. - Boundary reachability: worker runner instantiates the shipping Harness with each Preset; normal board and Goal requests reach this path, independently of demo/test code. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the model has substantive task execution/splitting discretion; deterministic runtime and human task publisher set only operating constraints. - Evidence: crates/harness3-core/src/engine.rs#L65-L185; crates/harness3-worker/src/runner.rs#L55-L135; crates/harness3-platform/src/scheduling/board/mod.rs#L277-L325. - Basis: explicit / structural. - Confidence: high for source path; no provider-backed E2E performed. - Caveats: external tools may be unavailable and effects require idempotency beyond checkpointing; Demo output is not real research evidence.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: resolve contention for the same operational work item and stabilize parent-child availability as peers execute and split work. - Distinct S1 units: separately provisioned model/tool Agent instances, each bound to a Run, Preset snapshot and independent local state. - Inter-S1 disturbance: competing peers could both treat the same pending task as theirs; child and parent attempts could race while a predecessor is unresolved, causing duplicate work or invalid integration. - Attenuating coordination relation: atomic claim checks current task state and Preset compatibility, rejects a second claimant; staged children only become runnable after a successful source Run, while parent waits until all children finish; failed children block the parent. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: a rejected claim returns a conflict to the Agent, pending/claimed state changes what may be read/claimed next, and completed children reoffer the parent for a new integrating Agent. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the specific multiple-owner race and dependent-work collision are regulated through conditional transactional ownership and state transitions, not just message delivery. - Disturbance / variety regulated: peer contention and out-of-order execution of shared task dependencies. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the first-party board contract exposes task claim/split/feedback to peers, but collision arbitration is deterministic; no autonomous regulator decides among competing peer priorities. - Decision owner: deterministic SQLite transaction for conflict arbitration; individual S1 Agents independently choose which compatible task to attempt. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: shared board, generation/Run identity checks, atomic UPDATE, dependency state, event log and bounded task count. - Closure path: successful claim gives one execution owner; unsuccessful claimant receives 409 and can choose differently; parent is made pending after children complete or blocked on failure. - Boundary reachability: board tools are automatically injected into regular task-board Agent runs, not a theoretical adapter; claim enforcement occurs before work tools are usable. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: coordination function and feedback path are real, but an autonomous S2 decision actor with cross-peer conflict discretion is not provided. The S2-specific constructor/claim primitive warrants C, not A. - Evidence: crates/harness3-platform/src/scheduling/board/mod.rs#L277-L325; crates/harness3-platform/src/scheduling/board/mod.rs#L333-L383; crates/harness3-worker/src/runner.rs#L18-L47; docs/task-board.zh-CN.md#L1-L39. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: this is narrow coordination of task ownership/dependencies, not general negotiation of conflicting goals, cost allocation, global fairness or distributed consensus.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A(P) - Function: regulate current operating capability/instance allocation against admitted work and shared resource limits, with distinct autonomous routing and human fleet-control modes. - Whole-system current view: pending board tasks, executable Preset catalog and model availability, active worker slots / reserved instances; in human fleet mode, reported capacity, fleet status and outstanding operations. - Current-control decision scope: choose which registered capabilities to provision for currently pending work; in parent mode change desired instance counts, scale or retire fleets under the current global quota. - Disturbance / variety regulated: uneven present task demand, incompatible/missing Presets, model availability, instance/worker scarcity and fleet size drift. - Decisive decision or feedback right: model router selects a Preset name/version/reason from the live eligible catalog for each actionable task; separately an authorized human chooses fleet target/termination. - Decision owner: autonomous bounded routing model in the base mode; legitimate operator in the distinct fleet mode. Deterministic Rust/SQLite performs candidate validation, quota, retry and reconciliation. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: catalog filter, route timeout/backoff, global worker/instance limits, fleet revision checks, operation receipts, reconcile loop. - Closure path: accepted router selection is provisioned into a real instance and Run; failed/no-capacity decisions defer task for another attempt. In parent mode operator target writes desired state and fleet reconciler creates/drains actual instances, with operation/status returned. - Boundary reachability: model-backed router is wired into normal worker loop when a real provider is configured; fleet scale/terminate commands and the reconciliation loop are standard local API/worker capabilities, not a roadmap. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the base model makes a nontrivial capability-selection decision within live constraints, whereas Rust only validates and provisions. Human fleet mode owns a different concrete current-resource choice; this does not make all enforcement an autonomous S3 policy. - Evidence: crates/harness3-platform/src/scheduling/routing.rs#L91-L235; crates/harness3-platform/src/scheduling/routing.rs#L264-L315; crates/harness3-platform/src/scheduling/board/mod.rs#L243-L275; crates/harness3-platform/src/control/fleets.rs#L76-L137; crates/harness3-platform/src/control/fleets.rs#L202-L270; crates/harness3-worker/src/scheduler.rs#L20-L60. - Basis: explicit / structural. - Confidence: medium-high for real-model routing and fleet closure; no paid-model E2E. - Caveats: the router is a deliberately bounded single-call classifier, not an unlimited supervisor; its discretion is narrow current Preset choice. Demo uses deterministic selection. The human mode manages fleets and does not grant a human co-ownership of the same automatic board route in one run.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independently inspect frozen operational contributions against specified checks, produce an evidence-linked judgment and drive bounded repair/acceptance gating. - Claim being audited: Goal work artifacts and final contributions support the specified acceptance/evidence checks. - Ordinary reporting path: operational S1 Runs and their artifacts/Goal contributions, including model-generated completion claims. - Complementary access path: a freshly allocated verification instance receives a frozen artifact manifest and separate paginated reader that checks hashes, read coverage and version; it does not inherit worker tools or writable workspace. - Independence boundary: new instance/session and reviewer-only evidence/report tools; different scoped role from original worker, even though both might use an LLM provider and the same operator config. - Who acts on findings: platform deterministically applies passed/failed/unknown/stale verdict states and, if preauthorized, launches bounded affected-task rework; human remains final Goal acceptor. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unsupported claims, stale shared context, missing/changed artifact evidence, quality defects and false success on incomplete Runs. - Decisive decision or feedback right: independent model reviewer judges each required check as passed/failed/unknown with reasons and evidence references, separate from the originating worker. - Decision owner: fresh autonomous reviewer Agent owns the audit judgment; deterministic coverage/immutability gates and rework rules enforce/act on that verdict. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: frozen hashes, per-artifact complete-read records, no plugin inheritance, bounded review timeout, manifest revision, immutable verdict, automatic rework quota and human acceptance. - Closure path: accepted verdict after successful reviewer Run becomes passed/changes-requested/inconclusive; failed findings can rerun related tasks and re-verify, while passed status gates later human acceptance. Stale evidence blocks acceptance. - Boundary reachability: optional verifier policy is supported on normal Goal and research workflows, and the worker runner launches its real configured Preset/model with restricted reviewer tools; it is not merely CI or Demo. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: semantic audit judgment comes from an independent Agent, not from the source worker or deterministic parser. Enforcement of complete reads does not magically prove external truth. - Evidence: crates/harness3-platform/src/collaboration/verification/mod.rs#L120-L263; crates/harness3-platform/src/collaboration/verification/mod.rs#L343-L616; crates/harness3-worker/src/runner.rs#L95-L135; docs/verification.zh-CN.md#L1-L42. - Basis: explicit / structural. - Confidence: high for shipped independent channel; medium for actual review quality without E2E. - Caveats: checks are chosen by the user; reads demonstrate manifest coverage and integrity, not independent verification of external facts. The shared task board alone does not activate this Goal verifier.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no first-party externally sensing prospective adaptation decision loop changing the platform&#39;s future capabilities. - Disturbance / variety regulated: prospective shifts in task mix, technology, model efficacy or external environment not shown as managed future threats/opportunities. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no agent-owned search/option/adoption loop with external future signal and return into Preset repertoire or system strategy. - Decision owner: not established; operator can publish new Presets, and individual S1s can do user-requested research. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: user-directed research template, Preset versioning, task router, shared context, verification/rework and plugin catalogue. - Closure path: existing Preset selection and repair react to present work; no demonstrated future distinction yields adopted capability change through an S4 route. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: planning, multi-agent research and selecting among already available Presets are not automatically S4. - Evidence: crates/harness3-platform/src/collaboration/research/mod.rs#L1-L130; crates/harness3-platform/src/scheduling/routing.rs#L91-L177; docs/managed-agents.zh-CN.md#L1-L115. - Basis: structural bounded absence. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: an operator could build an adaptive Preset pipeline but no in-boundary autonomous or parent-governed S4 closure is evidenced. ### Absence scope\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no runtime ultimate identity/constitutional arbitration loop beyond static, operator-authored Preset and platform policies. - Disturbance / variety regulated: potentially conflicting organizational values or who the collective is; no first-party escalation of such a dispute is traced. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no exposed agent or legitimate parent path from a raised identity-policy issue through authoritative judgment back into next operation. - Decision owner: operator authors immutable Presets and config; these are static parent-set constraints rather than an evidenced ongoing S5 loop. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: versioned Presets, tool binding/security checks, human Goal acceptance, model router restrictions and cancellation. - Closure path: ordinary task approval/review and preset publication changes normal capabilities, not established policy conflict resolution after a surfaced issue. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: authorizing a tool, passing an acceptance check or selecting a task specialist does not decide constitutional identity or ultimate policy. - Evidence: crates/harness3-core/src/policy.rs#L1-L22; crates/harness3-platform/src/scheduling/routing.rs#L137-L177; crates/harness3-platform/src/collaboration/coordination.rs#L135-L175; docs/managed-agents.zh-CN.md#L1-L115. - Basis: structural bounded absence. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: the human operator does set top-level deployment constraints, but no function-specific proposed/contested ultimate-policy closure is demonstrated merely by being able to configure them. ### Absence scope\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/2d0bcd786318bfe5e21d11d3c07a020adb044bba/assessments/ne/neopanic-harness.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 2d0bcd786318bfe5e21d11d3c07a020adb044bba\">2d0bcd7 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-31T23:56:39+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/dohwi/pipaya\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">pipaya</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-31\">2026-10-31</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/dohwi/pipaya/tree/dd5cdfd560c1499301a1f2fe4c07f9173a12d53a\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision dd5cdfd560c1499301a1f2fe4c07f9173a12d53a\">dd5cdfd &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Persistent model-backed workspace sessions perform coding/investigation tasks and can use disposable helpers. - Disturbance / variety regulated: User requests, file/command results, provider faults, context pressure and delegated observations. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose workspace tools, follow-up actions and final answers; choose whether to delegate or create a persistent peer. - Decision owner: The configured session agent, composed by the first-party SessionManager around Pi Agent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Pi inference/loop dependency, first-party tools, SQLite transcript/context state, queues, compaction, retry and limits. - Closure path: Prompt → constructed Agent with owned tool definitions → selected calls → observations/persistence → further turns and final response. - Boundary reachability: ensureLive creates Agent with the session&#39;s actual model, history, prompt and first-party tools. The packaged server and desktop-bundled server wire this composition directly. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The first-party product owns the concrete executable composition; Pi SDK is a dependency and does not donate uninstantiated higher functions. Without the session actor, server APIs and gates do not choose project work. - Evidence: [Session construction and helper execution][R1], [tools][R2], [server distribution][R3], [manifest][R4]. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: No provider-backed E2E run or Pi-wide capability claim is made.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: The supplied peer protocol attenuates reciprocal reply/notification oscillation between independently running sessions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Two peer sessions can repeatedly reply to one another or cause completion notifications to trigger further replies. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The receiving agent decides whether a peer response is needed and whether to end that exchange; the sender chooses explicit reply/notification requests. - Decision owner: The participating session agents own semantic response necessity; runtime-authored notification and send bounds constrain the exchange. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: peerMessage marks other-session origin and says to reply only if needed; completion watches are consumed once, reports say no reply needed, and per-run send overflow returns stop/report feedback. - Closure path: Peer message or one-shot completion report → recipient agent sees explicit reply/termination semantics → chooses further peersend only when needed, or stops; cap refusal feeds back to the sender&#39;s next action. - Boundary reachability: Peer tools are automatically included in normal session tool plans unless the operator disables them. PeerService wraps actual delivered messages and sends report feedback into prompt/followup paths of ordinary agents. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The narrow credited discretion is agent choice over necessary replies under a first-party anti-oscillation protocol. The generic mailbox, layout groups and delegation are not themselves the S2 witness, and the counter is not an autonomous owner. - Evidence: [Peer templates, send counter and one-shot watchers][R5], [tool descriptions][R2], [always-reachable peer tool wiring][R1], [tool plan][R6]. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: This is bounded structural attenuation, not proof that arbitrary agent conversations terminate. The counter resets each run and cannot alone rule out cross-run ping-pong or shared-file collisions.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A(P) - Function: A session agent can regulate current commitments across peer sessions, and the local human operator has a distinct supplied supervisory mode. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Peers are stuck, working on the wrong course, waiting on questions, or consuming the wrong model/active-session capacity. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Inspect all peer state, steer/abort work, create/close/resume sessions, withdraw queued work, change model/effort and answer blocked questions; in parent mode the operator makes those current-control choices through the server/client API. - Decision owner: Base mode: a model-backed session acting as peer supervisor. Parent mode: the local server operator using the administrator-authority client/API. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: peerlist/read/current watcher notifications, peersend steer/followup, peerabort/manage/create/answer, persisted session state and authenticated REST/WebSocket interfaces. - Closure path: Current peer view/report → agent or operator selects intervention → SessionManager applies queue/run/model/lifecycle/question changes → subsequent peer execution follows the changed current commitment. - Boundary reachability: Every normal session receives the first-party peer tools, wired to SessionManager host methods, unless disabled. The client/API exposes server-wide activity/session/status/usage and actual abort/message/patch/question endpoints for its operator. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Removing the supervisory actor leaves APIs, messages and limits but no discretionary selection of whom to stop, steer, resume or reconfigure. Parent control is evidenced separately from generic askuser approval. - Evidence: [Peer state/intervention methods][R5], [specific tool semantics][R2], [SessionManager host wiring][R1], [authenticated operator routes][R7], [server API contract][R3]. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: Authority is broad among sessions of one trusted server; this is neither a global organizational claim nor evidence that every run uses a supervisor.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: No shipped audit-specific independent challenge-and-corrective-control loop is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: A peer or helper may make an incorrect completion claim. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No supplied audit role/verdict/return protocol decides that claim independently. - Decision owner: Ordinary producing sessions report their work. Helper role files and extension logic are user supplied. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: peerread can inspect ordinary transcript/tool snippets; workspace read/grep and generic helpers are available; event/context/read tracing preserve evidence. - Closure path: Peer/helper output returns as ordinary tool text or messages, without an audit-specific independently controlled adjudication and corrective path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A fresh helper context and raw file tools can be used to build a reviewer, but the shipped delegate interface itself is generic operational delegation. No bundled reviewer is credited from an external marketplace. - Evidence: [Helper definition/execution][R8], [session delegate][R1], [peer read][R5], [extension boundary][R9]. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: Users can create a material complementary audit application; it needs its own independent owner, evidence-access and corrective-return proof.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No external-and-prospective adaptation loop is supplied. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Provider configuration, new skills/extensions, changing instructions and available upstream releases. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No first-party intelligence path develops future-relevant options and negotiates a return into current capability. - Decision owner: Users configure endpoints/extensions/skills and request updates; sessions perform requested present tasks. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: AGENTS.md/skill refresh, compaction, provider settings, extension APIs and release-tag updater. - Closure path: Settings apply next run; updater installs the latest release after operator request and running-session checks. No prospective environment model/adaptation-option decision loop is wired. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A latest-tag inequality check and package restart are maintenance, not external/future adaptation judgment. General capability extension and compaction are not S4; no parent adaptation mode is inferred from update consent. - Evidence: [Source update policy][R10], [live context/skills][R11], [SessionManager][R1], [extension skill boundary][R9]. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: The assessment does not claim that users cannot organize an adaptive workflow on this product.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No identity/ultimate-policy adjudication-and-return loop is supplied. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Session instructions, permissions, enabled skills/tools and broad server administrator control. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No identity-level issue is routed to ultimate authority and returned as an authoritative system policy decision. - Decision owner: Users author prompts/settings; trusted extensions run with server privileges; agents can change bounded peer metadata/current work. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Authentication token, frozen initial tool/base-prompt snapshot, live AGENTS/skill settings, extension approval and per-tool enforcement. - Closure path: Settings constrain subsequent calls, and askuser/peeranswer returns ordinary question answers. Those are not a supplied identity-policy adjudication protocol. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A field named purpose, a user answer and administrator control are not S5 without a function-specific ultimate-policy issue and governing return. - Evidence: [Context and instruction boundary][R11], [session controls][R1], [question lifecycle][R12], [operator API][R7], [peer metadata][R5]. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: The S3 parent mode is not reused as S5 evidence; external extension policy does not enter the first-party default boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/af57467e7adc84c630a0ffc6bb9cfb46e586a39f/assessments/pi/pipaya.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit af57467e7adc84c630a0ffc6bb9cfb46e586a39f\">af57467 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-27T21:17:31+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/rokartur/agent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">agent (rokartur)</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-27\">2026-10-27</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/rokartur/agent/tree/f02a473edd8a08368876e45a641f8ed8c2a4c85d\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision f02a473edd8a08368876e45a641f8ed8c2a4c85d\">f02a473 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: coding transformation by root and child agent loops - Disturbance / variety regulated: unknown source, tool failures, changing files and delegated findings - Decisive decision or feedback right: select tool/action/arguments and revise the approach from results - Decision owner: root/child models within their assigned scopes - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: provider adapters, permissions, tool dispatcher, JSONL persistence, snapshots and channels - Closure path: model tool choice → execution result → next model input; child answer/follow-up → root continuation - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Removing the model leaves execution support but no discretionary solution. - Evidence: source/src/agent/mod.rs#L539-L670, source/src/agent/subagent.rs#L188-L215, source/src/agent/subagent.rs#L263-L323 - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium-high - Caveats: Pinned source establishes a supported path, not observed effectiveness or universal deployment. - Boundary reachability: Agent::runturns is called by supported CLI/server modes; child launch uses the current installed executable.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: prevent stale writes from overwriting another operational writer&#39;s changes - Disturbance / variety regulated: root/parallel children can act on the same cwd after reading different file versions - Decisive decision or feedback right: reject stale anchored edits and return fresh-read feedback; broader coordination discretion still needs composition - Decision owner: first-party anchor controller enforces the rule; no separately assigned autonomous cross-writer coordination owner - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: session-specific anchors, reread/sync before edit, model-visible error and common child cwd - Closure path: another writer changes file → stale anchor rejected → caller receives ESTALEANCHOR and must reread before a valid subsequent edit - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A concrete collision-specific path exists; deterministic rejection does not itself own autonomous coordination judgment. C credits this narrow supplied path, not the ability to program any coordinator. - Evidence: source/src/tools/hashline.rs#L308-L329, source/src/tools/hashline.rs#L654-L659, source/src/agent/mod.rs#L975-L1004, source/src/agent/mod.rs#L640-L666 - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium-high - Caveats: Pinned source establishes a supported path, not observed effectiveness or universal deployment. - Boundary reachability: Hashline-backed tools are installed for the coding agents and child launch defaults to the root cwd. - Distinct S1 units: root coding agent and parallel child coding agents. - Inter-S1 disturbance: stale read-modify-write on shared files after a different writer edits them. - Attenuating coordination relation: anchor ownership plus reread/sync rejects stale range rather than silently overwriting. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: caller receives explicit fresh-read instruction in tool result, then chooses its next action. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the credited path is version-sensitive writer-conflict rejection, not child spawning, work ordering or a mailbox.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: current cohesion and accountability of the bounded child task team - Disturbance / variety regulated: different child states, failed/superseded work and model/effort commitments - Decisive decision or feedback right: inspect team roster/results, change active child scope, stop and resume commitments, select model/effort for replacement work - Decision owner: root model for its current team - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ListAgents, SendMessage, TaskStop, subagent state and follow-up queues; static concurrency cap is enforcement - Closure path: team status/result → root current judgment → steering/stop/resume/new scoped resource choice → changed child operations → later state/result - Why this is / is not agent-owned: This is narrower than global fleet governance and exceeds initial decomposition: a running child can be steered, stopped and resumed after accountability feedback. - Evidence: source/src/agent/mod.rs#L892-L1029, source/src/agent/subagent.rs#L92-L108, source/src/tools/tools.json, source/src/agent/swarm-prompt.md#L18-L35 - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium-high - Caveats: Task-team scope only. Generic human plan approval, session interruption and permission-mode edits are not credited as a separate parent S3 mode. - Boundary reachability: Root&#39;s shipped tool set exposes status and intervention; follow-ups enter its continuing run loop. No custom control-plane code is required. - Whole-system current view: root&#39;s roster of its child tasks/status plus returned answers, within the task-team recursion. - Current-control decision scope: ongoing team commitments, task scope, model/effort resources and intervention; not provider infrastructure or unrelated sessions.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: challenge delegated outcome claims with complementary primary-artifact access - Disturbance / variety regulated: a child answer can be mistaken despite appearing complete - Decisive decision or feedback right: independently open cited source or rerun the decisive command before relying on the child&#39;s claim - Decision owner: root agent as a decision holder distinct from the child producer - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: built-in swarm guidance embedded in Agent tool description, root Read/Bash, returned child claim and root continuation - Closure path: child answer → root selects checks on depended-on claims → raw artifact/command evidence → root revises or withholds next work and can steer/resume the child - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The shipped instruction explicitly treats the answer as a claim and names an alternate evidence path; judgment resides with the root, not a deterministic hook. This does not rely on naming an arbitrary spawned child &#39;auditor&#39;. - Evidence: source/src/agent/swarm-prompt.md#L18-L27, source/src/tools/mod.rs#L38-L43, source/src/agent/mod.rs#L1022-L1029, source/src/agent/mod.rs#L892-L920 - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium-high - Caveats: Prompt-guided supported audit mode, not evidence that every run performs it. Auto permission reviewer and deep-research&#39;s mandatory verify phase are not used to establish complementary audit. - Boundary reachability: The default bundled swarm prompt is injected into the root&#39;s Agent tool description; root already has source/command tools and consumes child results in supported modes. - Claim being audited: child&#39;s completion/finding claims on which the root&#39;s next step depends. - Ordinary reporting path: child final answer or queued background follow-up. - Complementary access path: root independently reads cited path:line or reruns decisive command against workspace reality. - Independence boundary: root context/judgment is separate from the child production context; model provider may be shared, so this is workflow/claim independence, not model-diversity assurance. - Who acts on findings: root changes its own next step or sends corrective child instructions.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: outside-and-then adaptation of the harness/team capability - Disturbance / variety regulated: Web research, long-term goals and self-update are not by themselves prospective system adaptation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No function-specific decisive path established. - Decision owner: None established for this function in the assessed mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: bundled deep-research/workflows, goal/todo/cron, model routing, compaction, update and skills - Closure path: No qualifying function-specific return into operations. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Deep-research produces the requested research report; updates follow published versions. No shipped loop turns external prospective distinctions into adaptation options and a current-capability regulatory return; parent adaptation was not established. - Evidence: source/src/agent/deep-research.js, source/src/update.rs, source/src/tools/goal.rs, source/src/agent/workflow.rs - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: identity / ultimate-policy closure - Disturbance / variety regulated: Ask/Auto/FullAccess permissions, trust and prompt policy bound ordinary tool actions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No function-specific decisive path established. - Decision owner: None established for this function in the assessed mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: permission reviewer, trusted project instructions, plan approval, settings, root swarm constraints and session UI - Closure path: No qualifying function-specific return into operations. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The review judges an individual proposed action against the existing request; it neither resolves identity-policy tension nor constructs an authoritative policy-return loop. Mutable prompts and contributor governance do not close S5 for the deployed task team. - Evidence: source/src/agent/permissions.rs#L178-L229, source/src/agent/mod.rs#L1106-L1141, source/src/agent/prompt.rs - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/28430b0a3ee80e366fca884d46873921a7deb469/assessments/ag/agent-rokartur.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 28430b0a3ee80e366fca884d46873921a7deb469\">28430b0 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-26T20:37:16+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/dtadpole/heddle\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Heddle</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-26\">2026-10-26</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/dtadpole/heddle/tree/6ae7e8dd80931c69946b3f1d75d523c77a99252d\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 6ae7e8dd80931c69946b3f1d75d523c77a99252d\">6ae7e8d &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: produce requested workspace/task results through an iterative model/tool process. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unknown files, tool observations, permission refusals, new user input and model/context limitations. - Decisive decision or feedback right: select the next tool, arguments, follow-up investigation or final answer in light of returned observations. - Decision owner: the model-driven root/teammate agent running runTurn; the operator supplies its objective and permitted environment. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Cordis loader, model adapter, context preparation/compaction, tool registry, sandbox, approvals, round limit and append-only session log. - Closure path: agent tool choice → tool output/refusal persisted in session → context prepared for the next model round → revised agent choice or completed result. - Boundary reachability: normal heddle CLI/Web modes use runTurn; the optional Team plugin also calls the same loop for teammate turns. No outside coding-agent CLI is needed for the witness. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: without the model&#39;s ongoing decisions, deterministic transport and tool handling cannot choose the task-specific next action. - Evidence: loop, Team plugin, profile. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: source evidence establishes an executable decision/action path, not model task quality or reliability; no live provider run was made.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: attenuate work-claim and ordering conflicts between distinct teammate S1 units on one team board. - Distinct S1 units: Lead and autonomous teammate turns, or two independently running teammates whose work shares the team task board and filesystem. - Inter-S1 disturbance: competing claims of one pending task, a stale task update after another teammate&#39;s change, or an attempt to start work before a dependent task is complete. Concurrent write-scope overlap is also surfaced as a warning, not a hard filesystem lock. - Attenuating coordination relation: version-checked claim/update, owner enforcement, dependency readiness, task views with scope-overlap warnings and direct teammate messaging. Agents can choose available work or revise task dependencies/claims after reading the resulting state. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: stale or occupied claims are refused with explicit error codes; the agent receives those tool results, can re-read the board and select a different claim or coordinate with another member before acting. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: compare-and-set revisions and ownership/readiness constraints explicitly address collisions among competing task actors; the returned conflict state changes which task work can begin. The mailbox alone would not suffice. - Disturbance / variety regulated: conflicting claims, out-of-date plans, blocked dependencies and overlapping teammate work. - Decisive decision or feedback right: select/revise task ownership and dependency handling in response to live conflict signals. - Decision owner: Team-mode agents acting through the model-facing task tools; fixed CAS and access checks only enforce their attempts. The Lead also has the supervisory reassign right separately considered under S3. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: durable team/task event fold, revision comparison, ownership/lead checks, dependency DAG validation, TEAMTASKSTALE/TEAMTASKBLOCKED refusals and notification/mail transport. - Closure path: multiple teammates read shared work → agent proposes claim/plan → service accepts or refuses based on current conflict/dependency state → result enters agent&#39;s next model round → agent adjusts work selection or message/claim; accepted updates become the next shared task state. - Boundary reachability: the shipped team plugin registers task tools in heddle.patch.yml and wires them to the actual team session log; no developer-only verifier or external coordinator is borrowed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: agents retain choice over which eligible tasks to claim, which conflicts to resolve by messaging and how to revise dependencies; removing their choices leaves only fixed admission guards, not a complete discretionary coordination response. This is a supported optional mode, not a claim that every single-agent run has S2. - Evidence: task state and CAS, model-facing task tools, Team service, policy note. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: write scopes are advisory; source supplies the decision and feedback channel, not an observed conflict-resolution quality result. An operator may direct a team, but no S2 parent notation is used.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: maintain whole-team current commitments and intervene across multiple ongoing task cells. - Whole-system current view: model-facing listagents and teamtasklist expose all members, their live/durable states, task readiness, owners, revisions and dependency blockers. - Current-control decision scope: the Lead can create and revise shared tasks/dependencies, reassign a teammate&#39;s claimed work, reopen work, stop an active teammate turn and send corrective instructions; ordinary teammates cannot reassign someone else&#39;s owned task. - Disturbance / variety regulated: an overloaded, blocked, stale or misassigned current team whose shared commitments cannot be resolved within one task cell. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose a whole-team assignment/priority/intervention based on the current board and member statuses, including interruption or reallocation. - Decision owner: the model-driven Team Lead in the first-party Team mode. Lead-only role checks and cancellation machinery enforce its chosen action; they do not choose the intervention. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: full team state fold, task ownership and revision gates, requireLead, turn registry and abort signal, serialized message delivery. - Closure path: Lead obtains team-wide status → decides to reassign/reopen/stop/message → Team service updates authoritative task/member operation or aborts a turn → affected teammate receives changed work/steered instruction and subsequent operation follows it. - Boundary reachability: first-party Team plugin registers listagents, teamtasklist, teamtaskupdate, sendmessage and interruptagent, with Lead authority and live turns support wired in the supplied profile. It does not depend on private contributor orchestration. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the Lead must select which live commitments to change; the runtime merely rejects illegal states and carries the selected change. A team-less profile has no S3 claim from this mechanism. - Evidence: Team service, task intervention rules, Team tools, profile. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: no claim of effective real-world team governance or independent current-performance metrics; hard limits and goal-round continuation alone would not establish S3. Operator instructions over ordinary tasks do not separately prove an S3 parent mode.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no material first-party complementary independent audit-return path established for the installed team/product at this boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: a teammate&#39;s potentially incorrect completion report or misleading task status. - Decisive decision or feedback right: an independently controlled judgment against direct operational evidence and subsequent corrective feedback is not a shipped Team-mode control loop. - Decision owner: the development-side verifier owners govern the separate project-checking process; runtime Lead/teammates can inspect and review but do not instantiate an independently governed complementary auditor by default. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: session/event logs, task status, developer verifiers, offline unit tests and review collection scripts. - Closure path: team task completion/status may inform routine Lead decisions; no separately controlled audit-of-reporting decision and return into Team control is established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a verifier for Heddle&#39;s own implementation and ordinary self-reported task completion are not independent inspections of teammates&#39; operating reality at this recursion. - Evidence: Team service, verifier boundary, review collection. - Basis: structural, bounded negative. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: a user can independently instruct a teammate to inspect raw artifacts, but general tool expressiveness does not establish the function-specific independence and returned control in the shipped operating mode.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no material prospective external-environment sensing, option development and return into installed team capability. - Disturbance / variety regulated: future model/tool/environment changes rather than current task context or failure recovery. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose adaptation of the installation&#39;s future capability based on externally derived options; no such shipped decision path is identified. - Decision owner: operator/developer can update plugins and profiles outside the running organizational loop. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: persistent session/context, context compaction, goal continuation, endpoint selection and dynamic Cordis plugin loading. - Closure path: goals continue present work and context summaries feed the next task round; neither supplies prospective external intelligence with adopted strategic adaptation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: context and goal maintenance are within-task operations; plugin extensibility is not itself an adaptation regulator. - Evidence: goal driver, context composition, boot/configuration. - Basis: structural, bounded negative. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: custom plugins or an operator&#39;s separate adaptation program would require a new declared boundary and evidence.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no material installed-instance identity or ultimate-policy resolution loop is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: potential identity-level tensions about purpose or ultimate permissible operation, distinct from an ordinary tool authorization. - Decisive decision or feedback right: legitimate ultimate-policy judgment with an authoritative return to subsequent operation; not supplied by Heddle&#39;s approval dialogs. - Decision owner: operator selects standing permissions and profiles; project maintainer owns the separate product-development goal, not a shipped identity-level governance loop. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: allow/deny/ask tool permissions, per-call approval, sandbox modes, configuration file and team-role rules. - Closure path: human approval or denial returns to one tool call; it does not carry an identity/ultimate-policy issue to a legitimate parent and return a durable policy resolution. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the model cannot revise ultimate identity through a first-party policy-authority path; operator&#39;s ordinary edit/approval is not automatically S5=P. - Evidence: approval service, sandbox policy, development goal. - Basis: structural, bounded negative. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: an operator&#39;s real organization can have S5 outside this local installed harness.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/cdef6102b9bb2fd020571ea0486c2be26ad22aec/assessments/dt/dtadpole-heddle.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit cdef6102b9bb2fd020571ea0486c2be26ad22aec\">cdef610 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-24T18:56:35+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/DMRLZZ/DaveCode\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">DaveCode</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-24\">2026-10-24</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/DMRLZZ/DaveCode/tree/271910253a005845174c2410dc15675877a9883e\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 271910253a005845174c2410dc15675877a9883e\">2719102 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: execute user-directed coding and goal/artifact work through model-owned next-action decisions and actual workspace/output feedback. - Disturbance / variety regulated: varying repository state, implementation alternatives, task dependencies, real tool outputs, failed quality gates, incomplete context, provider constraints and uncompleted goal artifacts. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose which first-party coding tools to invoke and what code/action to attempt next; for artifact roles, generate a task-specific output from received objective and dependency evidence. - Decision owner: the selected model-backed BuiltinExecutor actor and goal role agents in supported first-party mode. The runner selects/validates task transitions deterministically and does not inherit the model&#39;s coding discretion. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Router, quota/budget, WorkspaceTools, token/iteration ceilings, Git branch operations, task context, retry/repair policy, goal store and acceptance validators. - Closure path: current task/context → model requests repository tools → WorkspaceTools executes → exact tool result enters same conversation → next coding decision; goal artifact calls persist their output and return it to the subsequent goal review/task selection. - Boundary reachability: createRunner constructs the shipped built-in executor; wireProject exposes it through CLI/server; GoalOrchestrator.runCodeTask invokes the first-party runner while artifact work calls router.complete with first-party role prompts. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the model-backed code/role actor leaves graph selection, router/failover and verification machinery, but no discretionary construction of task-specific code edits or artifacts from evidence. - Evidence: executor.ts; runner.ts; orchestrator.ts; runtime.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high for shipped source construction; no provider-backed outcomes measured. - Caveats: only the native builtin composition supplies this positive S1 path. External CLI executor modes may have their own decision owner outside DaveCode.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: attenuate competing operations&#39; shared-worktree mutation conflict between the first-party standalone autonomous runner and first-party goal operation that invokes code tasks on the same project. - Disturbance / variety regulated: both top-level execution modes check out Git branches and commit in one writable workspace; simultaneous use would race on branch/index/worktree state. - Distinct S1 units: the standalone AutonomousRunner coding unit and the goal-directed role/code operational composition exposed as a separate first-party GoalService. They are distinct runnable operational entry points to one repository, not two model calls within the same tool step. - Inter-S1 disturbance: competing top-level runner and goal ownership of one Git worktree can cause Git checkout/commit interference. workspace-lock.ts explicitly identifies this conflict. - Attenuating coordination relation: wireProject instantiates one shared WorkspaceLock; the runner acquires it as owner runner, goal mode holds it as goal, a competing owner receives busy, and nested goal-owned code runner uses reentrant goal ownership. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: rejected launch returns RunnerError(&#39;busy&#39;)/GoalError(&#39;busy&#39;) to the parent CLI/API caller, preventing conflicting execution; subsequent initiation must occur after the holder releases the lock. A model-owned cross-unit rescheduler is not instantiated. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: this is not a DAG edge or role routing: a first-party lock is explicitly tied to a real shared-Git mutation disturbance between two separately startable coding/goal operating units and changes their admission behaviour. - Decisive decision or feedback right: grant or refuse exclusive entry to the shared writable-workspace operating boundary and report the conflict for later caller scheduling. - Decision owner: deterministic WorkspaceLock enforces exclusivity according to fixed rules; a developer/operator would have to compose an autonomous policy for what postponed work to start next. The shipped S2-specific feedback path supports constructor state C, not autonomous A. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ownership-counting WorkspaceLock; shared wireProject runtime wiring; runner acquireLock and GoalService.start; goal RunnerCodeTasks reentrant owner. - Closure path: goal/runner start proposal → shared lock grants or rejects → only one writer progresses; rejection returns busy to caller and later attempts may proceed after release. Autonomous adaptive cross-unit scheduling remains to be composed. - Boundary reachability: the published CLI project wiring always shares the lock between the first-party goal and standalone runner for that project; this does not depend on an unshipped plugin. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the conflict-specific first-party lock enforces a fixed partition and returns a decision, but a coding model does not autonomously decide who waits, retries or reallocates operations after a collision. - Evidence: workspace-lock.ts; goal/service.ts; goal/code-tasks.ts; cli/runtime.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium; the claim is limited to exclusive shared-worktree admission, not rich semantic task coordination. - Caveats: lock is in-process and does not prove cross-process or distributed exclusion. Alternative top-level execution modes may be seen as exclusive rather than co-active S1s; this is the principal semantic boundary to review before canonical admission.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A(P) - Function: regulate the present whole-goal task commitment population through reviewer-informed retries/new tasks and operator-managed task-graph priorities/constraints. - Disturbance / variety regulated: some goal tasks fail or become blocked, artifact/code outcomes vary, budget/task/round limits constrain current work and changed task priorities may require revised commitment selection. - Whole-system current view: goal reviewer receives every task&#39;s status, role, attempt count, title, notes and bounded actual artifact excerpt, plus budget context and current round. The CLI tasks listing and API expose the shared project task graph to operators. - Current-control decision scope: the goal reviewer decides when to retry failed work, which additional tasks enter the next round, and whether the bounded entire goal is complete. In operator mode, current project task priority/dependency/acceptance edits and status changes alter which pending commitment nextTask selects in later runner iterations. - Decisive decision or feedback right: revise current task commitments from whole-goal evidence and return the decision to task execution, or let legitimate project operator do so through task-graph control. - Decision owner: goal reviewer model actor for the autonomous A mode; project operator using first-party task list/edit/status API for the distinct P mode. Fixed task/round/token limits enforce bounds but do not own the discretionary choice. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: GoalOrchestrator.review, applyRetries/normalizeTasks, persisted GoalStore, task graph validation, nextTask, CLI/HTTP routes and fixed budgets/workspace locks. - Closure path: goal outcome/status/artifact excerpts → reviewer model produces retry/new task/finish judgment → orchestrator alters tasks and executes the next round. Separately, operator reads task graph → changes priority/dependencies/status → persisted project graph → next runner selection consumes the revised commitments. - Boundary reachability: first-party GoalService is constructed by createGoalService and wired into the published CLI/runtime; task listing/edit endpoints and runner graph reads are part of the same installed project mode. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: without the goal reviewer the deterministic DAG/limit machinery can walk given tasks but cannot make evidence-driven judgments to revise the current goal task portfolio. In operator mode the human makes those current-control decisions; the API only stores/enforces them. - Evidence: goal/orchestrator.ts; goal/service.ts; cli/commands/tasks.ts; server/routes/tasks.ts; brain/graph.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium-high for bounded goal-level S3; the operator mode is project-current-control only, not a claim of global DaveCode governance. - Caveats: a standalone runner&#39;s deterministic priority choice and stop button alone are not the autonomous S3 basis. The positive autonomous mode rests on the goal reviewer receiving whole-goal status and revising current commitments, not merely task decomposition.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: supply an optional independent acceptance judgment over actual staged code changes beyond the production actor&#39;s summary and configured deterministic check results, with corrective return. - Disturbance / variety regulated: coding actor may self-report completion despite a patch failing the task&#39;s acceptance criteria even when configured lints/tests pass. - Claim being audited: that the implementation diff fulfills each task acceptance criterion and can be accepted/merged. - Ordinary reporting path: built-in executor returns its summary and the Validator runs configured checks; without an independent configured judge, NoneJudge always passes after the checks. - Complementary access path: when runner.judge.kind=&#39;jev&#39; the first-party runner obtains actual staged Git diff/stat plus test validation, passes them and task acceptance to dedicated TypeSafe Jev via JevJudge, and receives a separate calibrated acceptance verdict. The alternative llm judge exposes the same inputs but its routing independence is not assumed. - Independence boundary: the Jev decision is made by an external dedicated evaluator on fresh raw Git diff/validation evidence with a separate model/service from the coding actor&#39;s implementation session, not by replaying the implementer&#39;s own success message. Source-level evidence supports the path; no live independence/accuracy experiment was run. - Who acts on findings: AutonomousRunner converts a failed judge verdict into a failure report for the subsequent implementation repair cycle, or marks the task FAILED when the configured retry limit is reached. - Decisive decision or feedback right: judge whether the actual change satisfies acceptance criteria independently of the coding actor&#39;s conclusion; determine acceptance or corrective retry. - Decision owner: the optional dedicated TypeSafe Jev evaluator model in the first-party jev supported mode; DaveCode&#39;s schema parser, confidence threshold, and runner enforce/transport its judgment without owning the evaluator&#39;s discretionary assessment. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: createJudge, acceptance inputs/diff capture, score threshold, zod response validation, validator, repair-pass state machine and bounded failure handling. - Closure path: candidate diff/test results → independent Jev assessment → pass reaches delivery or reject is incorporated into failure text → built-in coding agent sees report and repairs → fresh diff is rejudged. - Boundary reachability: createRunner selects createJudge(cfg.judge, ...) from first-party runner config, which permits jev; runTask invokes it after successful deterministic validation as part of installed CLI/goal-driven coding operation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the separate evaluator owns the acceptance judgment, while the runner and threshold gate enforce the returned verdict; it is not ordinary self-review by the production actor in this supported mode. - Evidence: autonomous/judge.ts; autonomous/create.ts; autonomous/runner.ts; config/schema.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium; optional independent judgment/return is clearly wired, but practical audit quality was not tested. - Caveats: default judge.kind=&#39;none&#39; has no independent audit; an llm judge on the same model route cannot be assumed independent. The A state credits the explicit first-party opt-in dedicated Jev mode only and does not imply all deployments instantiate it.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no first-party external-and-prospective organizational capability adaptation loop established within the supported project runtime. - Disturbance / variety regulated: quota exhaustion, model availability and current-task failures are detected; internal replanning/repair and operational failover respond to present conditions, not outside-and-then organizational capability evolution. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no separate prospectively sourced option to redesign current organizational capability with an adoption decision was established. - Decision owner: not established for S4. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: quota windows, provider route scoring/circuit breakers, goal plan/review rounds, retry/repair feedback and global/project memory. - Closure path: no externally/future informed capability-option generation → judgment/adoption → return into current organizational capability is wired; immediate failover and goal replanning return only into current operations. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: role planner and goal reviewer plan/revise task actions, while router algorithms choose among configured providers; none is evidenced as autonomous prospective redesign of DaveCode&#39;s capability repertoire. - Evidence: docs/ARCHITECTURE.md; goal/orchestrator.ts; engine.ts. - Basis: structural absence review. - Confidence: medium-high within the frozen source distribution. - Caveats: tracking provider quotas over future windows is predictive operational routing, not evidence of a distinct S4 capability-adaptation organization.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no first-party runtime identity/ultimate-policy adjudication loop established at the declared project harness boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: goal text, route settings, allowed tools, account policy, branch/merge modes, acceptance and operator task edits constrain execution but do not decide harness ultimate identity. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no identity/ultimate-policy issue is escalated, decided by legitimate authority and returned as a durable authoritative runtime policy revision. - Decision owner: not established for S5; operator/developer configuration sets constraints rather than closing a distinct VSM S5 function. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: DaveConfig, DAVECODESYSTEMPROMPT, CLI/API task edits, security checks, route settings, scoped workspaces and execution limits. - Closure path: no identity/policy issue → legitimate ultimate authority → authoritative decision → returned policy governing later operation is instantiated. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: planner/reviewer/task owner decisions concern the task portfolio, not the identity of the coding organization; static config and permission gates do not exercise S5 authority by themselves. - Evidence: config/schema.ts; goal/orchestrator.ts; server/routes/tasks.ts. - Basis: structural absence review. - Confidence: high within runtime boundary. - Caveats: this negative is not a statement about maintainer governance for future DaveCode releases.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/e1e4706466182cc143e13deb1db6b560aa529b3d/assessments/da/davecode.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit e1e4706466182cc143e13deb1db6b560aa529b3d\">e1e4706 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-30T16:01:52+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/paper-instruments/feather-train\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Feather</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-30\">2026-10-30</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/paper-instruments/feather-train/tree/cdb5f34bad11c0c75ca4124b25a3e5da86bb62e2\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision cdb5f34bad11c0c75ca4124b25a3e5da86bb62e2\">cdb5f34 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: execute user knowledge work and child assignments into deliverables. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing file contents, incomplete sources, tool errors, model-response interruptions and task-specific analysis. - Decisive decision or feedback right: select the next tool/action and decide how returned evidence changes the work or final answer. - Decision owner: parent/child model agent within its assigned task. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: tool schema validation, local file/shell/MCP transport, turn caps, retries, compaction and saved traces. - Closure path: model action → tool invocation → ToolMessage → next model decision → deliverable/final answer. - Boundary reachability: CLI run calls runlocal; the packaged local runner constructs FeatherAgent for parent and children. No developer-only dogfood actor is borrowed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the model leaves transport and caps but no task-specific discretionary next action. - Evidence: runner, local wiring. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: source-backed availability, not an empirical claim of deliverable quality or long-run viability.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: prevent the operational team from overwriting shared deliverables or duplicating one another&#39;s work. - Distinct S1 units: parent and independently executing child assignments, including multiple concurrent children. - Inter-S1 disturbance: agents with separate histories/read revisions can edit the same shared file from incompatible prior versions; duplicated assignments can also consume effort on the same work. - Attenuating coordination relation: injected parent guidance assigns allowed files and one assembling writer for a shared workbook/document/deck; child guidance preserves others&#39; edits and prohibits writing files another agent is changing. The parent chooses these scopes and revises work through follow-ups/stop. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: statuses/reports enter the parent&#39;s next model turn; follow-up assignments specify revised work. File tools return stale-read rejection into the offending agent&#39;s next turn. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the shipped guidance specifically addresses concurrent same-file interference, chooses exclusive assembly responsibilities and prohibits overlapping writes; this is additional to generic spawn/wait. - Disturbance / variety regulated: shared-file ownership and stale views across distinct operational agents. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose who may write which deliverable and reconcile overlapping work before further changes. - Decision owner: the autonomous parent agent; children apply their scoped instructions locally. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: assignment/report tools, separate revision maps, stale-read checks. Guidance is not an atomic lock and shell writes can bypass file-tool checks. - Closure path: assignment with allowed paths → child action/report → parent reconciliation/follow-up → subsequent scoped work; stale file-tool edits return an error requiring current evidence. - Boundary reachability: runlocal appends PARENTGUIDANCE, supplies CHILDGUIDANCE through Subagents, and exposes team tools in the supported production mode. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the model chooses the actual partition and corrective instruction; deterministic revision checking only rejects a preselected invalid write. - Evidence: coordination guidance, parent wiring, file-tool revision feedback, scripted shared-file test. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: a supported behavioral regulation path, not proof that every model follows it or that simultaneous shell writes are impossible. Single-agent mode has no inter-unit coordination demand.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: regulate current commitments of the task team and intervene when a child is unnecessary, incomplete or no longer useful to the whole deliverable. - Whole-system current view: parent context plus listagents statuses, selected waitagent reports, failed/exhausted partial-work diagnostics and compacted team-state reminders. - Current-control decision scope: decide active assignments, restrict scope/side effects, follow up on gaps, stop owned children and decide which completed work supports the overall result. - Disturbance / variety regulated: incomplete/failed children, conflicting findings and currently active commitments competing for bounded concurrency. - Decisive decision or feedback right: revise commitments and terminate a selected child after reviewing present team state; this goes beyond initial task decomposition. - Decision owner: autonomous parent model. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: configured max-concurrent/total/turn limits, status registry, cancellation and awaited resource cleanup. The host owns fixed ceilings; these are not themselves autonomous resource bargaining. - Closure path: current reports/status → model selects follow-up/stop/new work → manager changes child lifecycle → results/status feed the next parent decision. - Boundary reachability: standard production team exposes all five team tools to the parent and constructs children; local runner owns the manager lifetime. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing parent discretion leaves fixed caps and automatic teardown, but no evidence-dependent choice of which commitment to revise or stop. - Evidence: model-facing team tools, status/follow-up, stop/cleanup, parent responsibility. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: one local task team&#39;s current control, not organization-wide portfolio control or authority to change host ceilings. The noninteractive CLI and general embedding API supply no distinct function-specific human-parent mode.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no material independent complementary audit path in the assessed task-team distribution. - Disturbance / variety regulated: erroneous operational claims are handled by ordinary checks and parent result reconciliation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no separate audit judgment/feedback owner established. - Decision owner: ordinary producing agents and parent remain responsible for QA. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: execution traces, artifact reads and test tools provide observability. - Closure path: routine checking stays in production; no independent audit-to-control loop is supplied. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a child can be asked to check work, but the generic assignment API supplies no specifically independent auditor role or complementary audit contract. - Evidence: subagent responsibility and reporting, adjacent grading, external Harbor adapter. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: a host can construct a different audited system.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no shipped external-and-prospective adaptation loop at the local team boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: web/source information and model/context issues are processed as current task work. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no adaptation-options-to-current-capability decision path established. - Decision owner: host selects configuration; producing model handles tasks. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: optional web search/fetch, skills, compaction and separate training recipe. - Closure path: source retrieval enters task context; no distinct prospective capability-regulation conversation is supplied. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: generic research or model training alone does not establish S4. - Evidence: supported runtime composition, Train&#39;s external recipe, package separation. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: future custom research assignments are not bounded absence counterexamples.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no runtime identity/ultimate-policy resolution loop established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: task scope, source trust and host constraints are applied as preselected guidance. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no identity-level case → legitimate ultimate authority → returned policy path supplied. - Decision owner: host/user sets task and instructions; ordinary parent model controls current task work. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: system guidance, profile validation, filesystem boundaries and configured limits. - Closure path: constraints govern tools; no distinct ultimate-policy decision-return loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: being the root task agent or accepting host instructions does not grant ultimate-policy authority. - Evidence: production guidance, CLI config/run, supported embedding limits. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: an operator&#39;s ability to replace instructions is not itself function-specific parent governance.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/48be0b607dc2399a7f268e985f4645b26679a84a/assessments/fe/feather.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 48be0b607dc2399a7f268e985f4645b26679a84a\">48be0b6 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-23T03:07:03+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/whoops-1/termagent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">TermAgent</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-30\">2026-09-30</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/whoops-1/termagent/tree/33aa966551fcc2c050c0a213642d9966c3dd4a21\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 33aa966551fcc2c050c0a213642d9966c3dd4a21\">33aa966 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: perform coding work by interpreting the current objective, selecting permitted repository/tool actions, executing them and revising subsequent actions from returned evidence. - Disturbance / variety regulated: source/worktree state, implementation alternatives, provider uncertainty, tool/build/test failures, context pressure, no-progress/repeated actions and changing task evidence. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the next task-specific tool/action and revise it from tool/provider/workspace feedback. - Decision owner: the model-backed TermAgent actor in each foreground or background Agent runtime. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: tool registry; provider abstraction/routing; sessions; context budgeting/compaction; permissions; profiles; execution workflow; retry/no-progress guards; repository map; skills/plugins/MCP. - Closure path: task/current context → model decision → first-party tool execution → result persisted/returned → same actor selects another action or final response. - Boundary reachability: the shipped CLI, headless/server surfaces and detached workers instantiate the same first-party Agent/tool runtime. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the model actor leaves workflow/permission/session machinery but removes the open-ended choice of what repository action to perform next; deterministic mechanisms constrain and transport rather than replace the coding decision. - Evidence: src/agent/agent.ts; README.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: particular non-read actions can be parent-gated in ask, but TermAgent also ships auto as a first-party autonomous permission mode.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: attenuate destructive interference among multiple simultaneously active subordinate coding S1s sharing one project by bounding concurrency and enforcing non-overlapping writable workspace scopes. - Disturbance / variety regulated: concurrent background coding agents could edit the same or nested paths, overwrite one another, create non-reviewable shared-state races or exceed the bounded worker capacity for one session. - Distinct S1 units: each parallelagents task becomes a separately persisted agent task with its own detached worker process, provider configuration, prompt, status/output and declared scopePaths. - Inter-S1 disturbance: simultaneous agents share the project filesystem; overlapping file/directory scopes create a concrete mutation conflict risk. The design documentation explicitly names concurrent agents editing the same files as the conflict being prevented. - Attenuating coordination relation: the primary actor chooses 2–4 tasks and declares paths for each; parallelagents normalizes those paths, rejects out-of-project scopes, rejects pairwise overlaps, checks overlaps with already active agent scopes, enforces a maximum of four active workers and repeats the admission check under a cross-process session lock. Scoped workers are prevented from writing outside their declared region. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: accepted scopes constrain child write behavior; rejected overlap/capacity calls return errors to the primary model, which can redesign task decomposition/scopes or run work sequentially. Active scoped commitments are persisted and considered by later admissions. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the mechanism is expressly conditional on a concrete inter-S1 interference class—overlapping concurrent workspace mutation—and alters admission/write behavior specifically to attenuate it. Mere multiplicity or task routing is not the basis of the positive state. - Decisive decision or feedback right: select which child S1 tasks may be launched together and assign their non-overlapping workspace ownership scopes; after scheduler feedback, revise those task/scope choices. - Decision owner: primary model-backed TermAgent actor in approvals=&#39;auto&#39;. Interactive ask can parent-gate launch authorization, but Methodology 0.3.x does not encode parent-mode notation for S2. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: parallelagents; scope normalization/overlap checks; activeAgentTasks; per-session capacity; cross-process session lock; scoped worker write checks; durable task records. - Closure path: primary actor decomposes work and proposes tasks/scopes → scheduler compares requested/current active scopes → launch is admitted or rejected → accepted children operate inside constrained scopes / rejection returns conflict evidence → subsequent primary decomposition can change. - Boundary reachability: parallelagents is registered in the standard CLI/server runtime together with first-party worker/task machinery; no application-authored coordinator is required. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic code enforces safety once task/scopes are proposed, but without the primary model there is no task-specific choice of which independent coding commitments to create together or how to partition their work. The supported auto mode closes that discretion without parent approval. - Evidence: src/tools/parallel.ts; src/tasks/manager.ts; docs/PHASE3EXECUTION.md; src/tools/permissions.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: S2 is deliberately bounded to declared workspace-scope interference; it does not imply generic semantic coordination among agents whose declared scopes are disjoint. Human-gated ask runs remain supported, but S2 has no (P) publication state in Methodology 0.3.x.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A(P) - Function: regulate the current population of durable subordinate coding commitments by deciding what background work to start, observing its current status/output and terminating commitments that should no longer continue. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing current workload decomposition, independently progressing subordinate tasks, failed/stuck/unneeded workers, bounded concurrent capacity and the need to stop or redirect current commitments while foreground work continues. - Whole-system current view: each launch returns the durable IDs of the subordinate commitments that form the current bounded worker population; taskstatus exposes a selected task&#39;s durable status, PID, output, session identity, provider and scope state, while launch admission itself queries the complete active-agent set for the session before admitting more work. The foreground actor can therefore inspect the status/output of every commitment it created and receives scheduler feedback against the current active population. - Current-control decision scope: the primary actor can create independent durable coding commitments through backgroundagent or a bounded set through parallelagents, choose their prompts/scopes, and terminate queued/running commitments through model-facing taskcancel; these actions change the current subordinate workload population rather than merely the content of one S1 turn. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decide which new coding commitments should exist now, what scoped work they own, observe their current results/status, and cancel commitments after that feedback. - Decision owner: primary model-backed TermAgent actor in approvals=&#39;auto&#39;; interactive operator becomes decisive for shell-risk start/cancel authorization in approvals=&#39;ask&#39;. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: backgroundagent; parallelagents; taskstatus; taskcancel; TaskManager durable records/events; worker PID lifecycle/recovery; active-agent population query; capacity/session locks; permission gate. - Closure path: current task IDs/status/output and admission feedback → primary model decides start/parallel/cancel action → permission path → task manager spawns or signals worker → durable task state changes → subsequent taskstatus/scheduler/model turns observe that changed current population. - Boundary reachability: all lifecycle tools and worker/task machinery are first-party standard runtime components registered on supported CLI/server surfaces; the child workers reconstruct TermAgent&#39;s own provider/runtime state. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: in auto, removing the primary model leaves lifecycle machinery and fixed limits but no task-specific judgment about which commitment to create/cancel or how to decompose current work. In ask, the parent operator owns final authorization for the same commitment action. - Evidence: src/tools/background.ts; src/tools/parallel.ts; src/tasks/manager.ts; src/tools/permissions.ts; README.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: there is no model-facing one-call tasklist; the positive current view is bounded to the commitment IDs returned to the parent plus per-task taskstatus and scheduler feedback over the complete active session set. This is sufficient for the small S3 claim but should not be read as a rich organization-wide dashboard.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no material boundary-reachable complementary audit role with sufficiently independent access, audit judgment and corrective return was established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: ordinary implementation defects and failed verification are regulated inside the production workflow, but no separate S3-specific audit disturbance/claim channel was established. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established for a complementary auditor. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: deterministic verifyproject; separate verification provider routing; workflow verification phase; tests/build commands; task status and ordinary repository inspection. - Closure path: absent at S3 level. The enforced planning/building/verifying/iterating controller routes ordinary production evidence back to the same coding workflow; no materially independent claim-access path and audit actor is required for that closure. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a role-specific provider does not by itself create an independent auditor, and deterministic verification results are ordinary operational QA. No separate actor was found whose audit judgment challenges the production actor through complementary evidence and then returns to corrective control. - Evidence: src/agent/workflow.ts; src/agent/agent.ts; docs/PHASE3EXECUTION.md. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: an application could potentially create an audit-specific background agent with custom instructions, but uninstantiated composition is not credited as first-party closed S3.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no material first-party external-and-prospective adaptation loop over TermAgent&#39;s own present organizational capability was established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: repository-map retrieval, external/provider switching, skills/plugins/MCP, sessions and role-specific routing can improve current execution but do not close an outside-and-then capability-adaptation function. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: provider/router profiles; fallback providers; repository map; skills/plugins; MCP; project instructions; session/context compaction. - Closure path: no environmental/future distinction → adaptation-option generation/evaluation → adopted change to present organizational capability/S3 loop was established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: current provider routing and retrieval choose among preconfigured capabilities for the task; they do not let a first-party S4 actor prospectively redesign/adopt organizational capabilities from external/future evidence. - Evidence: README.md; src/agent/agent.ts; docs/PHASE3EXECUTION.md. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: developer/operator addition of providers/plugins/skills changes future capability but is adjacent configuration work unless the running organization itself closes that prospective adaptation decision.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no material first-party runtime identity/ultimate-policy closure was established at the assessed recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: approval modes, profiles, tool risks, scoped-worker restrictions, project instructions and configuration constrain current operation but do not constitute an ultimate-policy/identity decision loop. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established for S5-level identity or ultimate policy. - Decision owner: not established at S5; developer/operator configuration sets the operative envelope outside a qualifying runtime identity closure. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: PermissionGate; approval config; build/plan/explore profiles; workflow phase rules; scoped worker prohibitions; project instructions; provider/plugin/MCP configuration. - Closure path: absent at S5 level; no identity/ultimate-policy issue → legitimate ultimate authority → authoritative decision → returned policy governing subsequent operation was found. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: models act within configured policy but do not own the legitimate right to redefine TermAgent&#39;s identity or ultimate rules. Operator approval of a launch/tool call is ordinary parent governance at that function, not S5. - Evidence: src/tools/permissions.ts; src/config/config.ts; README.md. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: project/repository governance determines future releases but remains adjacent to the runtime boundary and is not imported as product S5.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/80571eb4795983f28168746a9ce20d3a4df460c6/assessments/te/termagent.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 80571eb4795983f28168746a9ce20d3a4df460c6\">80571eb &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-27T17:42:55+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/gvergnaud/bise\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">bise</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-27\">2026-10-27</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/gvergnaud/bise/tree/2a0cf0caf75cb17e9b6ce40e90bab517e963e425\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 2a0cf0caf75cb17e9b6ce40e90bab517e963e425\">2a0cf0c &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Autonomous task execution - Disturbance / variety regulated: Uncertain code state, tool results and user requests - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose next tool/action and task completion report - Decision owner: Provider-backed main/task agent - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Bend tool dispatch, sessions, compaction, bash/edit/MCP and report persistence - Closure path: Tool results re-enter the agent loop; changed files and terminal report are returned to main/user - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Provider-backed main/task agent; A is architectural availability, not measured success; no provider was invoked. - Evidence: bend/runtime/main.bend; bend/runtime/repl-live.bend; rust/switchboard/src/prompts.rs lines 115–206 - Basis: structural - Confidence: high - Caveats: A is architectural availability, not measured success; no provider was invoked. - Boundary reachability: Bend provider/tool loop and durable hub; Rust switchboard role construction, devflow, task/control commands, inbox and worktree machinery wired into shipped terminal/desktop clients. The cited decision path is shipped in this mode and returns through Bend tool dispatch, sessions, compaction, bash/edit/MCP and report persistence.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: Prevent destructive interference between concurrent task agents - Disturbance / variety regulated: Two task units editing the same files or observing half-finished changes - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose disjoint files, sequencing, shared place or isolated worktree based on the actual tasks - Decision owner: Main agent - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: sb task board, spawn/place commands, worktree creation and landing machinery - Closure path: Main&#39;s placement decision determines the directories and integration paths used by subsequent worker actions - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Main agent; Git isolation alone is enforcement; the shipped main role supplies contextual coordination discretion. - Evidence: rust/switchboard/src/devflow.rs lines 393–425; rust/switchboard/src/prompts.rs lines 124–145 - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium - Caveats: Git isolation alone is enforcement; the shipped main role supplies contextual coordination discretion. - Boundary reachability: Bend provider/tool loop and durable hub; Rust switchboard role construction, devflow, task/control commands, inbox and worktree machinery wired into shipped terminal/desktop clients. The cited decision path is shipped in this mode and returns through sb task board, spawn/place commands, worktree creation and landing machinery. - Distinct S1 units: Concurrent task agents producing separate operational outcomes. - Inter-S1 disturbance: Two task units editing the same files or observing half-finished changes - Attenuating coordination relation: Choose disjoint files, sequencing, shared place or isolated worktree based on the actual tasks - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: Main&#39;s placement decision determines the directories and integration paths used by subsequent worker actions - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: Git isolation alone is enforcement; the shipped main role supplies contextual coordination discretion. Prevent destructive interference between concurrent task agents\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A(P) - Function: Current control over the project task team - Disturbance / variety regulated: Blocked commitments, changing constraints and capability allocation among live tasks - Decisive decision or feedback right: Inspect current team and revise briefs, constraints, agent model or intervention; alternatively user redirects team tasks - Decision owner: Main in base mode; legitimate local project operator in parent mode - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Injected bisestate/taskstatus, sb tasks/send/model, inbox and correction messages - Closure path: Reports/questions reach main; main&#39;s revised instruction or model applies to subsequent task turns; user decisions are returned by addressed correction/answer - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Main in base mode; legitimate local project operator in parent mode; Not inferred from the word orchestrator or from spawning alone. Parent current intervention is confined to this one project hub. - Evidence: rust/switchboard/src/prompts.rs lines 124–154; rust/switchboard/src/devflow.rs lines 402–445; README.md control/talk-to-any-agent - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium - Caveats: Not inferred from the word orchestrator or from spawning alone. Parent current intervention is confined to this one project hub. - Boundary reachability: Bend provider/tool loop and durable hub; Rust switchboard role construction, devflow, task/control commands, inbox and worktree machinery wired into shipped terminal/desktop clients. The cited decision path is shipped in this mode and returns through Injected bisestate/taskstatus, sb tasks/send/model, inbox and correction messages. - Whole-system current view: The cited current state/report path covers all units in the declared deployment. - Current-control decision scope: Inspect current team and revise briefs, constraints, agent model or intervention; alternatively user redirects team tasks\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: No separate complementary audit supplied for this boundary - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unreliable worker completion claims - Decisive decision or feedback right: No independently owned corrective-audit right established - Decision owner: None established - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Task reports, ordinary tests, history and optional generic review tasks - Closure path: Main is explicitly told to accept done reports and not re-run their tests; failed/blocked/no-check reports receive ordinary control - Why this is / is not agent-owned: None established; Development proofs and routine worker QA do not establish independent operational audit. - Evidence: rust/switchboard/src/prompts.rs lines 126–127 and 198–205; bend/laws/runtime.bend - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No shipped external-and-prospective adaptation loop - Disturbance / variety regulated: Changing future tool/provider/environment suitability - Decisive decision or feedback right: No first-party prospective option-to-capability decision loop established - Decision owner: None established - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Plugin installation, per-agent model selection, schedules, compaction and self.reload - Closure path: These support present operation or software updates; no supplied actor couples environmental futures to changed current capability - Why this is / is not agent-owned: None established; An arbitrary research task could be commissioned, but generic task/tool expressiveness is not an S4 constructor. - Evidence: bend/runtime/selftools-pure.bend; rust/switchboard/src/prompts.rs; README.md - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No identity/ultimate-policy adjudication loop supplied - Disturbance / variety regulated: System identity or ultimate policy in dispute - Decisive decision or feedback right: No function-specific ultimate-authority decision/return protocol established - Decision owner: Operator sets ordinary configuration; no qualifying S5 owner established - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Role prompts, permissions, inbox approvals, saved PR/trunk flow - Closure path: Flow choice and notification responses govern operational integration; they do not themselves establish identity-level or ultimate-policy deliberation - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Operator sets ordinary configuration; no qualifying S5 owner established; A real human decision path was considered; its matters are ordinary task/shipping controls, not automatic S5. - Evidence: rust/switchboard/src/devflow.rs lines 402–445; rust/switchboard/src/prompts.rs lines 150–156; prompts/prompt-bise.txt - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/ac6d6474ac3a34f33e646cd534adb2f22017c1d9/assessments/bi/bise.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit ac6d6474ac3a34f33e646cd534adb2f22017c1d9\">ac6d647 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-26T19:57:09+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Oklahomawhore/AgentHarness\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">AgentHarness</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-26\">2026-09-26</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Oklahomawhore/AgentHarness/tree/6bedcf45fad75e21df5e5cac827a898ef5c7d228\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 6bedcf45fad75e21df5e5cac827a898ef5c7d228\">6bedcf4 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: perform user-directed engineering work through repeated model-selected tool actions and feedback. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing user objectives, workspace/process state, tool outcomes and failures, model context pressure, follow-up input, child outcomes and external tool/provider responses. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the next substantive tool/action and revise the next step from returned evidence, or conclude the turn. - Decision owner: the active Agent model in the root Session; child models own the same local operational discretion within delegated child Sessions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Agent driver, Session event log, tool registry/execution scheduler, sandbox and approval services, LLM adapters, compaction/retry hooks and persistence. - Closure path: admitted user/session context → model request → assistant tool call/action → runtime execution → ordered tool/result / durable event → next model step observes the result and chooses a revised action or final response. - Boundary reachability: the Agent loop and core tools are mounted by the standard base-backed TUI/Web/headless product compositions. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the model removes the task-specific choice of tool, arguments, sequencing and stopping point; deterministic runtime machinery validates, schedules and executes those choices but does not make materially equivalent operational judgments. - Evidence: docs/agent-lifecycle.md; packages/bundle/base/cordis.patch.yml. - Basis: explicit + structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: attenuate interference among concurrently operating child Agents sharing the same workspace by deciding which delegated work is sufficiently independent to overlap and by revising/steering work when current child state requires coordination. - Disturbance / variety regulated: sibling child Sessions can run concurrently against one shared workspace or external resources and can race or invalidate one another when their effects overlap. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose task decomposition and whether multiple child commitments are independent enough to launch together; subsequently message or interrupt continuable children as coordination needs change. - Decision owner: the parent Agent model. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: parallel-safe delegation execution, separate child Sessions/identities, ordered parent result commit, durable continuable child identities, sendmessage, interruptagent, settlement notices and maxParallelToolCalls enforcement. - Closure path: parent model identifies independent work → emits multiple subagent delegations together (or keeps dependent work sequential) → children operate concurrently in separate Sessions against the shared environment → settlement/status/message evidence returns to parent context → parent may steer/interrupt/redelegate or change its own subsequent work. - Boundary reachability: the base bundle mounts the continuable subagent tool and its fixed model-visible guidance to &quot;Start independent delegations together&quot;, along with the follow-up control surface; the shared-workspace race is an implemented/shipped behavior rather than test-only topology. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the scheduler treats delegation as concurrency-safe and cannot infer whether free-text child tasks have disjoint workspace effects; the project explicitly leaves sibling workspace coordination to the model. Removing that model judgment leaves only unconditional overlap/caps, not materially equivalent task-specific coordination. - Evidence: packages/subagent/tool-subagent/README.md; packages/subagent/tool-subagent-control/README.md; packages/bundle/base/cordis.patch.yml; .agents/notes/archived/feature/2026-08-09-parallel-subagent-delegations.md. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: high - Caveats: the runtime does not provide a write-lease conflict solver; S2 credit is for the model-owned independence/timing/steering relation, not for the generic mailbox or parallel scheduler. - Distinct S1 units: root Agent plus two or more separately persisted child-agent Sessions, each with its own model/tool operation and durable identity. - Inter-S1 disturbance: concurrent sibling children can race on shared workspace files or external resources and thereby invalidate or interfere with peer work. - Attenuating coordination relation: parent-model decomposition/independence judgment determines what is co-scheduled; continuable children can then receive model-selected follow-up steering or interruption. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: child settlement notices, listagents status and accepted messages/interrupts feed current child outcomes into later parent decisions; follow-up input changes child execution at a later step boundary. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the credited relation is tied to the repository&#39;s explicit shared-workspace sibling-race disturbance and changes which child commitments overlap or are subsequently steered; Task/Room messaging alone is not credited.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: maintain current control over the root Agent&#39;s active continuable child organization: create commitments, observe the current descendant portfolio and intervene in live child work. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing child lifecycle state, completed/failed/idle/running commitments, new information requiring follow-up, runaway or no-longer-useful child work, and current need to change delegated effort. - Decisive decision or feedback right: create children with task/persona/tool/model/depth commitments, inspect the continuable descendant tree, send task-changing follow-up input and interrupt a child&#39;s current turn. - Decision owner: the parent Agent model for its current child organization. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: durable child catalog and Session identities, continuation manager, live Agent registry, authorization/lineage checks, listagents, sendmessage, interruptagent, settlement notices and depth/capacity enforcement. - Closure path: parent creates current child commitments → listagents/settlement notices expose current descendant status → parent chooses follow-up, interruption, additional delegation or its own integration action → runtime delivers/enforces the choice → subsequent child/root operation changes. - Boundary reachability: the shipped base bundle mounts continuable spawn children and all three model-facing control tools; no custom downstream controller is required for this mode. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: lifecycle and authorization services enforce identity and interruption, but they do not decide which child should be started, redirected or stopped for the current objective. Those task-specific current-control decisions belong to the parent model. - Evidence: packages/bundle/base/cordis.patch.yml; packages/subagent/tool-subagent/README.md; packages/subagent/tool-subagent-control/README.md. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: high - Caveats: listagents intentionally excludes one-shot children. S3 is established by the supported continuable-child mode, where the root can enumerate the complete continuable descendant tree; this does not claim equivalent supervision over every optional child mode. - Whole-system current view: for the continuable-child mode, listagents(scope=descendants) walks the root&#39;s complete continuable child tree and reports durable identity, direct parent/depth and current running, idle or ready status; settlement notices add outcome/final-message feedback. - Current-control decision scope: create/withhold child commitments; select task, route/persona/tool/depth settings where supported; send new work/steering to direct continuable children; interrupt current child turns; launch further work or integrate returned outcomes.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no material shipped complementary-audit function was established that independently challenges operational reporting or completion through a distinct access path. - Disturbance / variety regulated: potential inaccurate child claims or hidden operational failure were considered, but the standard distribution does not close an independent audit loop for them. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established for S3. - Decision owner: none established at the assessed boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Session/event durability, task status, approval logs, ordinary child transcripts/status and optional development evidence infrastructure provide observability or validation but do not form an independent complementary audit function. - Closure path: no qualifying independent audit finding → organizational response closure was found in the shipped mode. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: ordinary parent result/status observation is the in-band S3 path; no separate autonomous auditor or shipped audit judgment path owns a complementary verdict. - Evidence: packages/subagent/tool-subagent-control/README.md; docs/subsystems/development-room.md; .agents/notes/archived/feature/2026-08-02-session-search-not-shipped-default.md. - Basis: explicit + structural absence -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no material first-party external-and-prospective organizational adaptation loop was established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: future environmental/capability change can be researched through ordinary tools and shared Task context, but no shipped mechanism converts such intelligence into a governed change of the harness&#39;s present capability/organization. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established for S4. - Decision owner: none established at the assessed boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: MCP/tool discovery, development-evidence providers, skills, settings, Task publications, model-route selection and compaction alter current task information/configuration but do not close a prospective adaptation function. - Closure path: no qualifying external/prospective option → capability-change → subsequent-operation loop was found. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: current-task research and configuration choices remain S1/support behavior; they do not grant an actor organizational adaptation authority. - Evidence: packages/bundle/base/cordis.patch.yml; docs/subsystems/development-room.md; repository collaboration/evidence package surfaces. - Basis: structural absence -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no material runtime identity / ultimate-policy closure is established inside the assessed harness organization. - Disturbance / variety regulated: sandbox, approvals, system instructions, model/tool settings and profile composition constrain operation but do not constitute an S5 identity/policy issue handled by ultimate organizational authority and returned into operation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established for S5. - Decision owner: none established for S5 at this recursion. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: system prompt assembly, sandbox policy, user approval, model/provider settings, profile/bundle configuration and Task context authority boundaries. - Closure path: no qualifying identity/ultimate-policy issue → legitimate authority → authoritative policy decision → returned operational change loop was found. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the main model acts under configured identity and permission constraints but does not own the ultimate right to redefine the organization&#39;s identity or governing policy. - Evidence: packages/bundle/base/cordis.patch.yml; docs/agent-lifecycle.md; approval and collaboration boundaries described in repository documentation. - Basis: explicit + structural absence - Confidence: high - Caveats: ordinary user approvals and configuration edits constrain actions but Methodology 0.3.6 does not promote generic approval/configuration to S5 or parent-governed S5.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/41869737c521e1cc01cf533dc57e55ada3450f2c/assessments/agentharness-oklahomawhore.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 41869737c521e1cc01cf533dc57e55ada3450f2c\">4186973 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-01T23:09:23Z\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Yangtianspike/suisui-code\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SuisuiCode</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-02\">2026-10-02</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Yangtianspike/suisui-code/tree/c9017b0613b22151325a5904a521aefe4c62abbc\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision c9017b0613b22151325a5904a521aefe4c62abbc\">c9017b0 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: transform a coding objective into repository/process actions and a task result through iterative model-selected tool use with environment feedback. - Disturbance / variety regulated: repository state, implementation alternatives, tool/process outcomes, context pressure, provider responses, permission outcomes and user corrections. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the next substantive coding/delegation/tool action and revise subsequent action from returned evidence. - Decision owner: the configured model actor in the first-party main Agent loop; spawned child Agent instances own the same task-local operational discretion for delegated S1 work. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: tool registry/executor, permission engine, context manager and compaction, session persistence, iteration bounds, hooks, MCP adapters, TaskManager and worktree machinery. - Closure path: user objective/current context → model chooses a tool/action → SuisuiCode executes it → actual tool/environment result is appended to the conversation → the same model receives that result and chooses another action or a final answer. - Boundary reachability: suisuicode constructs this shipped provider-backed Agent directly; built-in coding tools and standard subagent runtime are wired by cli.py without requiring an external orchestration framework. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: if the model actor is removed while deterministic permissions, tool execution, context management and persistence remain, those mechanisms can enforce constraints but cannot choose open-ended coding actions. The substantive operational discretion is therefore agent-owned. - Evidence: README.md; src/suisuicode/cli.py; src/suisuicode/agent/agent.py; src/suisuicode/agent/runtocompletion.py; src/suisuicode/agent/agenttool.py. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: model inference is supplied by an external provider. The finding credits SuisuiCode&#39;s first-party loop that places the model in the operational decision role, not provider-side internals.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: attenuate workspace interference among distinct delegated coding S1 cells by assigning an isolated Git worktree execution root to subagents whose standard definition requests worktree isolation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: concurrent coding agents operating against one checkout can overwrite or observe one another&#39;s in-progress file changes and lose clear ownership of a change set. - Distinct S1 units: the main Agent tool can instantiate multiple independent child Agent loops, including the shipped full-tool general-purpose role and fork/user/project roles, with separate conversations/runtime state and foreground/background lifecycles. - Inter-S1 disturbance: mutating child agents can otherwise act against the same repository workspace, coupling independent work through shared files and stale observations. - Attenuating coordination relation: Definition.isolation exposes the explicit worktree mode; AgentTool routes such a child through executewithworktree, which creates a dedicated worktree and runs the child&#39;s full model/tool loop under that worktree cwd. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: the isolation result changes the actual filesystem root seen by that child; the injected worktree notice also requires local rereads before editing, so subsequent S1 file decisions/actions occur against the isolated checkout rather than the parent&#39;s live workspace. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the credited relation is specifically an interference-attenuation mechanism for concurrent mutating S1s. Generic background delegation, TaskManager state and mailbox surfaces are not by themselves used as S2 evidence. - Decisive decision or feedback right: bind an operational child to a dedicated worktree so its mutable workspace is separated from other S1 work. - Decision owner: no shipped autonomous coordination actor owns that isolation choice as an organizational policy. The developer/operator selects it in the subagent definition; first-party runtime then deterministically enforces the relation. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: subagent-definition parser; worktree manager; unique worktree creation; withcwd; worktree context notice; auto-cleanup/preservation reporting. - Closure path: a standard subagent definition specifies isolation: worktree → the model delegates work to that role → SuisuiCode creates a dedicated worktree → the child Agent executes with that worktree as cwd → its later reads/writes remain separated from the parent&#39;s checkout → cleanup preserves or removes the isolated branch/worktree according to result state. - Boundary reachability: isolation: worktree is an explicit first-party definition field accepted from the standard user/project subagent-definition surfaces and executed by the ordinary model-facing Agent tool. The user supplies the role/configuration but does not have to invent the isolation path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the main model&#39;s discretion leaves the same definition-selected worktree relation available to any invocation; the decisive coordination policy is not autonomously selected by a shipped coordinator. The S2-specific constructor is therefore C, not A. - Evidence: src/suisuicode/subagent/definition.py; src/suisuicode/subagent/parser.py; src/suisuicode/agent/agenttool.py; src/suisuicode/agent/agentworktree.py; src/suisuicode/subagent/builtin/general-purpose.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: none of the inspected built-in role files makes worktree isolation the autonomous default; the positive finding is intentionally constructor-level. The frozen Team mode is not used to strengthen this state because its teammate execution path does not close reliably.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: regulate the current population of delegated operational subagents by deciding which work to spawn, observing all background-run status/activity, cancelling work and re-tasking retained agents. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing workload decomposition, long-running or failed delegated work, redundant/obsolete current tasks, child-agent completion and the need to redirect follow-up work while the main objective is still active. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decide whether/what to delegate, which child role/model/task to instantiate, whether a running task should be cancelled, and what follow-up task should be sent to a completed named agent. - Decision owner: the autonomous main model actor, because Agent, TaskList, TaskGet, TaskStop and SendMessage are registered as model-facing tools in the ordinary first-party composition. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: TaskManager&#39;s task registry/status/result/activity accounting; background asyncio lifecycle; name registry; completion queue; tool schemas; deterministic cancel/restart execution. - Closure path: main model delegates via Agent → child Agent runs as an independent S1 under TaskManager → TaskManager records current status/activity/result → model can inspect the population through TaskList/TaskGet → model decides to wait, stop or issue follow-up work → TaskStop cancels or SendMessage restarts the retained child conversation, changing subsequent current operation. - Whole-system current view: TaskList returns all tracked background tasks, with id, name, status, tool count and last activity; TaskGet adds result/error, timing and usage. At the declared delegated-subagent recursion this is a current view of the operational child population managed by the main agent. - Current-control decision scope: the main model can create operational commitments through Agent, terminate an active commitment through TaskStop, inspect completion/failure, and assign follow-up work through SendMessage; these decisions alter which S1 work remains active and what retained agents do next. - Boundary reachability: cli.py registers the Agent, TaskList, TaskGet, TaskStop and SendMessage tools in the ordinary shipped main-agent registry, and AgentTool/TaskManager instantiate and track the same first-party child Agent loops; no Team-mode path or application-authored supervisor is required. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: TaskManager itself only records/enforces lifecycle transitions. Without the main model actor, the registry can list or cancel only preselected ids but does not decide which current work should exist, be stopped or be re-tasked. The substantive supervisory discretion belongs to the model. - Evidence: src/suisuicode/cli.py; src/suisuicode/agent/agenttool.py; src/suisuicode/task/manager.py; src/suisuicode/task/tools.py. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: this S3 finding is the ordinary functional SubAgent/TaskManager composition, not the richer advertised Coordinator/Team composition. SendMessage resumes completed named agents rather than modifying an already-running child&#39;s prompt; a currently wrong run is instead stopped and replaced/redelegated.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no material first-party complementary operational-audit loop with sufficiently independent access, audit judgment and corrective return is established at the assessed recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: permission checks, hooks, tests, worktree isolation and lead-side result inspection provide safety/visibility, but none closes a materially independent audit channel over current operational S1 claims. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no distinct first-party auditor is shown owning an independent approve/reject/escalate judgment over the operating subagent population. - Decision owner: not established for S3. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: permission engine; lifecycle hooks; task result/status inspection; worktree cleanup; ordinary user review; repository tests. - Closure path: not applicable for the negative finding. - Claim being audited: no distinct operational claim is assigned to an independent first-party auditor in the standard runtime. - Ordinary reporting path: child results/status/tool activity return through TaskManager and ordinary model/tool results to the same main supervisory agent. - Complementary access path: not established. Hooks are configurable extension points supplied by operator/project configuration, while worktree and permission checks are inline execution/support mechanisms rather than a separate route to operational reality. - Independence boundary: no qualifying shipped autonomous auditor with materially different access from the ordinary lead/task path was found. Development tests and user review remain outside runtime S3 ownership. - Who acts on findings: the main model or human can react to normal task/tool results; there is no distinct S3 finding-and-return closure. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: autonomous main/subagents can inspect work, but the same operating/supervisory actors do not become a complementary audit function merely by reviewing their own outputs. - Evidence: src/suisuicode/task/tools.py; src/suisuicode/agent/agent.py; README.md. - Basis: explicit + structural negative search. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: a downstream project can configure hooks or specialist agents to perform review, but generic extensibility does not establish first-party S3.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A - Function: convert durable project/user distinctions learned from completed work into model-selected long-term operating guidance that changes later SuisuiCode sessions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: recurring user corrections, stable preferences, project architecture/conventions, ongoing work/decisions and reference knowledge that later tasks would otherwise need to rediscover or could repeatedly mishandle. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decide whether observed information is worth retaining long term and whether to create, update, merge or delete project/user notes, including what How to apply guidance future work should receive. - Decision owner: the autonomous model actor invoked by the first-party memory-update loop. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: asynchronous per-turn trigger; existing-index/note retrieval; JSON action parser; project/user Store persistence; MEMORY.md index; startup loadindex; system-prompt assembly. - Closure path: a main coding turn completes → Agent.run extracts the recent turn and schedules memory.Manager.updateasync → memory model compares recent evidence with existing memory and selects create/update/delete actions → deterministic stores persist the chosen long-term guidance → a later SuisuiCode startup loads the memory index → that memory enters the main Agent system prompt and changes subsequent operating context. - Boundary reachability: the memory Manager is constructed by the standard CLI, passed into the main Agent, and invoked automatically after completed main turns when a provider is configured; persisted memory is loaded again by the same standard CLI path. - External distinction: actual conversation/project evidence includes user corrections/preferences and project knowledge learned during repository-facing work rather than only internal scheduler state. - Future / prospective distinction: the updater is explicitly told to retain only information worth remembering long term; feedback is framed as information to remember to avoid the same future error, while project notes encode ongoing goals/conventions and future How to apply guidance. - Adaptation option generated: the memory model autonomously generates structured create, update or delete actions and the content/type/level of each durable note, merging old and new evidence when appropriate. - Path back into current capability / S3: persisted notes are indexed by the memory stores; cli.py loads that index at startup and passes it as memorytext to the main Agent, whose system prompt uses it on later coding work. The returned adaptation therefore changes the information and operating guidance available to present S1/S3 decisions in later sessions. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic stores apply a structured action, but they do not decide what is strategically worth remembering, whether old guidance is obsolete, or how future work should apply a distinction. Removing the memory model leaves persistence machinery but no adaptation judgment. - Evidence: src/suisuicode/agent/agent.py; src/suisuicode/memory/manager.py; src/suisuicode/memory/prompts.py; src/suisuicode/cli.py. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: the freshly written memory is not shown being hot-reloaded into the already-running main Agent&#39;s cached memorytext; the established return path is durable adaptation into later startup/session operation, which is sufficient for the prospective loop but should not be read as same-turn adaptation.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no runtime identity/ultimate-policy tension-and-resolution loop is established at the chosen recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: permissions, modes, system prompts, subagent definitions, coordinator instructions and memory constrain or inform operation, but none is shown resolving a dispute about the organization&#39;s identity or ultimate policy. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established for S5. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: permission modes/rules; user approvals; configuration; system/subagent prompts; Coordinator feature flag; memory/instructions; hook policies. - Closure path: not applicable for the negative finding. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: models make task, supervisory and memory-adaptation decisions, but no first-party actor is given ultimate authority to resolve identity/policy tensions and return such a resolution into subsequent organization-wide operation. - Evidence: README.md; src/suisuicode/cli.py; src/suisuicode/coordinator/init.py. - Basis: explicit + structural negative search. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: human control over permissions or feature/configuration choices is ordinary parent/operator control over implementation and task execution; it is not promoted to S5 without an identity/ultimate-policy function.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/8a372087e865279eca3307563c52720f7ad8c4b2/assessments/suisuicode.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 8a372087e865279eca3307563c52720f7ad8c4b2\">8a37208 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-26T00:36:42+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/zybuu-ai/abhed\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Abhed</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-26\">2026-09-26</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/zybuu-ai/abhed/tree/151b8ddceee7ae232e526b3c237356151dde780e\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 151b8ddceee7ae232e526b3c237356151dde780e\">151b8dd &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: transform an open-ended technical objective into repository/environment outcomes through repeated model-selected actions, tool execution and evidence feedback. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing user requirements, repository/filesystem/process state, tool results/errors, model/provider responses, policy outcomes, context pressure and child-agent results. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the next substantive response/tool action and revise later behavior from observed tool/environment evidence. - Decision owner: the active Abhed model agent in the primary or delegated child loop. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Loop, tool registry, policy/approver, session workspace, event recorder/store, context offload/compaction, sandbox, provider adapter, budgets and presentation/server surfaces. - Closure path: objective enters the session → model selects an action/tool call → Abhed policy/runtime executes or refuses it → result/denial is recorded and returned to the conversation → later model turn observes changed evidence and chooses the next action → task/environment state changes. - Boundary reachability: app/main.go wires the standard interactive/headless Community Edition session directly to Loop and its tool surface; spawned subagents instantiate the same first-party loop with bounded role-specific configuration. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic code executes, constrains and records already-selected actions, but removing the model eliminates the substantive next-action choice and adaptive interpretation of returned evidence. - Evidence: internal/agent/loop.go; app/main.go; internal/agent/subagent.go. - Basis: explicit + structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: attenuate destructive write interference between distinct concurrently executing child agents by separating editing work into isolated git worktrees/branches when parallel writes would collide. - Disturbance / variety regulated: two or more writing subagents operating in one checkout can overwrite one another, obscure which changes survived and make concurrent implementation state inconsistent. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decide whether a concrete parallel batch requires worktree isolation rather than a shared workspace and supply the task decomposition/roles executed under that coordination choice. - Decision owner: the primary Abhed model agent calling the standard tasks tool. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Tasks schema/description, git worktree creation, per-child SubagentRequest.Workspace, child session scoping, branch naming, concurrency semaphore and returned per-worktree diff/merge instructions. - Closure path: primary agent identifies independent parallel work with write risk → selects tasks with isolation: &quot;worktree&quot; → runtime creates one branch/worktree per child and binds each child S1 to its own workspace → children operate without cross-writing → tasks returns every child summary plus branch/change/merge facts → parent uses that coordinated result for subsequent integration or further work. - Boundary reachability: app/main.go registers agent.Tasks in the normal Community Edition tool registry and supplies the standard SubagentFactory; isolation is a documented model-callable argument rather than a test-only helper. - Distinct S1 units: two or more fresh-context child Loop instances spawned concurrently by one tasks call, each capable of model/tool operation on a bounded delegated outcome. - Inter-S1 disturbance: parallel writing children sharing one checkout can overwrite one another&#39;s changes so the parent cannot reliably tell whose change survived. - Attenuating coordination relation: model-selected worktree isolation gives every child a separate git branch/checkout and filesystem scope, preventing one child from modifying another child&#39;s current work or the main tree. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: workspace binding changes each child S1&#39;s operational environment before it acts; after completion the parent receives per-branch status/change/merge information and can choose how subsequent integration work proceeds. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the positive mapping rests on an explicit inter-S1 write-collision disturbance and a first-party isolation relation built specifically to attenuate it; concurrency limits and subagent delegation alone are not credited. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Abhed deterministically enforces the requested isolation, but the parent model owns the task-specific judgment that concurrent writing work needs isolation and invokes the coordinating mode. Removing that judgment leaves the mechanism but not the actual coordination decision for the current task. - Evidence: internal/agent/parallel.go; internal/agent/paralleltest.go; app/main.go; internal/agent/subagent.go. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: high - Caveats: isolation: &quot;none&quot; deliberately permits shared-workspace operation. S2=A credits the standard agent-owned coordination path when parallel writes require isolation; it does not claim every concurrent batch is automatically coordinated.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no material first-party whole-system inside-and-now current-control loop is established over the temporary Abhed multi-agent organization. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Abhed bounds spend, spawn count and concurrency and the parent chooses initial delegation, but no standard model-callable path manages changing live child commitments from a whole-system current view. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established for ongoing cross-child current control. - Decision owner: none established for S3 at this boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: parent-created task batch, per-child maxturns, hierarchical token/subagent limits, MaxParallel, context cancellation and final aggregated task outcomes constrain execution but do not themselves establish S3. - Closure path: not applicable; the parent launches delegated work and receives results after child completion, but no reviewed path closes live whole-system status → discretionary intervention/reallocation → renewed whole-system feedback while children are active. - Whole-system current view: not established. task blocks on one child; tasks launches the requested batch and waits for all goroutines before assembling the returned outcome report. - Current-control decision scope: the parent can choose initial tasks, roles, turn caps and isolation, but the standard tool surface exposes no child list/progress/steer/interrupt/close/reassign controls over the running batch. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: without an established S3 function, ownership classification does not proceed. Initial decomposition and deterministic limit enforcement remain S1/S2 support rather than a whole-system current regulator. - Evidence: internal/agent/subagent.go; internal/agent/parallel.go; config/config.go; app/main.go. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review - Confidence: high - Caveats: the architecture document calls the primary actor an orchestrator and describes plan/budget ownership, but Methodology 0.3.6 requires the actual current-control function; naming, initial delegation and configured resource caps are insufficient.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independently inspect current repository reality for correctness defects through a fresh-context read-only reviewer child and return evidence-backed findings for corrective action. - Disturbance / variety regulated: implementation defects or inaccurate/incomplete conclusions that the implementing primary/child agent&#39;s normal execution and self-report may fail to expose. - Decisive decision or feedback right: form a correctness judgment from direct read/search access to the workspace and identify concrete bugs/failure scenarios independently of the implementer&#39;s transcript or summary. - Decision owner: the model running the built-in review subagent profile. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: fresh subagent context, review profile instruction, read-only read/glob/grep subset, independent child Loop, bounded summary return and parent model continuation. - Closure path: implementation/current repository state exists → parent invokes a review child through task or tasks → reviewer independently reads/searches repository artifacts and forms correctness findings → bounded findings return to the parent as a tool result → parent can revise code, test, delegate or otherwise change subsequent operation from those findings. - Boundary reachability: review is a built-in first-party profile exposed through the normal task / tasks tool schemas, and those tools are registered in the ordinary Community Edition CLI/headless runtime. - Claim being audited: that the current implementation/repository state produced or accepted by ordinary agent operation is correct enough to proceed without a concrete correctness defect. - Ordinary reporting path: an implementing agent/subagent returns its own assistant summary and normal tool/session record to the parent/runtime. - Complementary access path: a separate review child starts with a fresh context and directly inspects current workspace files through read/search tools rather than consuming only the implementing worker&#39;s summary. - Independence boundary: the reviewer receives none of the parent&#39;s conversational turns, has an explicit non-fixing review role and a read-only tool subset; its correctness judgment is produced by a distinct child model loop over repository evidence. - Who acts on findings: the primary Abhed model agent receives the reviewer summary as a normal tool result and owns subsequent corrective tool use, testing or further delegation within the same session. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Abhed supplies the separate review channel and tool restrictions, but the substantive bug judgment is made by the reviewer model from direct evidence. Removing reviewer-agent judgment leaves read/search primitives but no equivalent correctness findings. - Evidence: internal/agent/prompt.go; internal/agent/subagent.go; app/main.go; docs/guide/15-hawkeye.md. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: high - Caveats: HawkEYE and repository eval/red-team infrastructure are not the decisive S3 owner. The positive claim is the standard fresh-context direct-workspace reviewer path; arbitrary use of a generic subagent as a reviewer would not have been sufficient without this built-in role/access boundary.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no material first-party outside-and-then organizational adaptation loop is established at the assessed session/deployment recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Abhed can retrieve current information, search the web, use skills, switch models and retain session/history state, but these capabilities are not shown owning prospective adaptation of the harness organization. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established for adopting an organizational capability/policy change from external/future distinctions. - Decision owner: none established for S4 at this boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: web search, RAG/retrieval, skills, provider/model selection, project memory, session replay/fork/recall, compaction and repository-development eval/benchmark material. - Closure path: not applicable; reviewed paths acquire information or change task execution/configuration but do not close external/future sensing → adaptation option → adopted change to current organizational capability/S3. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the model may autonomously research information for an S1 task, but no separate S4 organizational adaptation function is established, so task research is not reclassified as S4. - Evidence: internal/agent/prompt.go; app/main.go; README.md. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no material first-party identity/ultimate-policy decision loop is established at the assessed session/deployment recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: system prompt, project/org memory, permission modes, deny/ask/allow rules, managed config and approval gates constrain operational behavior but are not shown resolving identity/ultimate-policy issues. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established for an identity-level policy issue. - Decision owner: none established for S5 within the Community Edition runtime. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: layered system prompt/memory, managed configuration precedence, ordered policy engine, absolute deny, destructive-action confirmation, human approver and workspace/additional-directory boundaries. - Closure path: not applicable; no reviewed path detects an identity/ultimate-policy issue, escalates it to legitimate ultimate authority and returns an authoritative resolution that governs subsequent operation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: agents act within operator/org-configured constraints and can receive ordinary action approvals/denials, but neither static policy nor generic approval confers ultimate policy authority on the model or operator as an S5 loop. - Evidence: docs/guide/04-permissions.md; config/config.go; internal/agent/prompt.go; app/main.go. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review - Confidence: high - Caveats: managed org policy can be stronger than local configuration and interactive approval can return reasons to the model, but Methodology 0.3.6 does not treat constraint precedence or ordinary action authorization as S5 without identity/ultimate-policy closure.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/609935e136cc3df4e59276eb2d9baf5b866c4334/assessments/abhed.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 609935e136cc3df4e59276eb2d9baf5b866c4334\">609935e &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-28T22:55:14+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/rootkiller6788/dsh-flow\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">dsh-flow</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-28\">2026-10-28</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/rootkiller6788/dsh-flow/tree/d11ad4f04400fda9c58cf2b9b5a11fa971818eed\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision d11ad4f04400fda9c58cf2b9b5a11fa971818eed\">d11ad4f &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: perform user-goal work in the actual project environment through multiple scoped member model-agent sessions; captain may also carry a bounded task, but its principal credited role here is team control. - Disturbance / variety regulated: varying implementation/research/verifier tasks, changing file/tool outputs, failed commands, partial results and review findings. - Decisive decision or feedback right: selected member model chooses tool/actions and reports task outcome within the assigned contract; the host supplies the model/tool engine, the plugin supplies live delegation, scoped task/attempt identity and return to team. - Decision owner: the operating captain/member model sessions, instantiated as DSH host continuable agents for this plugin&#39;s selected team; deterministic scheduler, rules and adapter do not choose the semantic work solution. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: createSubagentsRunner, host continuable child API, model route inheritance, task claims/attempt capability, assigned dependency results, inbox, state logs and completion evidence gate. - Closure path: approved team → scheduler dispatches ready task to member session → model acts in workspace → flowupdatetask and message return structured evidence → team state/captain observes → next work or review task. The host executes individual tools; first-party dsh-flow installs the closed team feedback relation. - Boundary reachability: runner: subagents is the default mount and /dsh-flow plus flowapprove initiate actual member execution, with tests exercising scheduler/host adapters. Manual mode is expressly non-executing. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: without the member&#39;s model decision there is no task-specific implementation/verification result; locks, dispatch and signatures alone would only manage empty work tickets. - Evidence: subagents runner mounting host agent, scheduler claim/dispatch, assignment prompt/tool output, supported team command. - Basis: structural, supported by fake-host scheduler/subagent tests. - Confidence: high for first-party team wiring, medium-high for external host runtime reachability without a live DSH model session. - Caveats: host/model provider remains separate; a pure runner: manual installation has no active S1, and an imported .agent-teams record is read-only visualization rather than new execution.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: attenuate destructive interference among multiple member S1 cells attempting incompatible writes or dependent work during a shared team goal. - Disturbance / variety regulated: file write collision, duplicate task claiming, unfinished attempt interleaving and premature dependent dispatch between separately operating team members. - Distinct S1 units: independently operating, named member model sessions with separate task/attempt identities and potentially competing paths in one workspace. - Inter-S1 disturbance: two independently acting implementation/repair members may claim overlapping workspace paths and overwrite each other&#39;s output; two task claims can also invalidate one member&#39;s existing attempt. - Specific interference / oscillation: concurrently open implementation/repair tasks can declare overlapping inScope paths and corrupt/overwrite each other&#39;s work; duplicate task claims or a member taking a second task before finishing its first can interleave commitments and lose the correct attempt. - Attenuating coordination relation: the shared first-party task graph refuses overlapping live writer scopes, withholds dependent tasks, and serializes member claims with identity-bound attempts. - Coordination response: validateCreateTask rejects overlapping scopes and explains serialize these tasks or split the paths; dependency readiness blocks downstream work until predecessor success; createMemberQueues and task locks ensure one active claim per member/attempt. Reassignment revalidates handoff after interruption. - Decisive decision or feedback right: first-party deterministic rules decide that an incompatible graph/task claim is inadmissible; captain/member may then revise work scopes or dependencies after the error, but the enforced anti-collision verdict is constructor-owned, so the state is C rather than A. - Decision owner: shipped rule constructors gates.js/assignment.js plus scheduler serialization for the S2 verdict; model captain remains owner of a revised plan, not of the invariant. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: scope-overlap matcher, team lock, member serial queue, dependency graph, attempt ID/capability, delivery state and error response. - Closure path: proposed overlapping tasks/competing claim → deterministic rejection or blocked readiness → visible tool error/status to captain and members → corrected serial/decomposed plan or later ready dispatch; later task work respects the new non-overlapping graph. This is a concrete interference/attenuation path, not generic messaging. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: the captain receives the explicit scope-conflict error and must split/serialize the plan; after a dependency finishes, the scheduler makes only newly eligible nonconflicting work available to the next member turn. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: a concrete overlapping-writer interference is recognized and prevented at the team boundary, with refusal and corrected task eligibility affecting what distinct S1 cells can actually do next. - Boundary reachability: gates and claim selection are invoked by normal flowcreatetask/flowupdatetask and default subagents scheduler, not only a test fixture. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the model from the overlap test leaves the same deterministic reject/serialized admission decision; model-generated alternatives support adaptation but the decisive S2 collision protection is constructed in code. - Evidence: live overlapping writers refused, dependency/owned-task selection, per-member double-claim lock, task reassignment locking. - Basis: explicit code-level invariant and structural execution path. - Confidence: high for deterministic protection; not a measurement of all real-world file races. - Caveats: scopes rely on accurate captain/model-declared paths; unmodeled side effects and external writers remain outside these protections. A blocked task requires legitimate correction; the gate alone does not invent the right new plan.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: let a team captain regulate the whole currently operating group—member commitments, task ownership, progression, failures and interventions—while members absorb local implementation variety. - Whole-system current view: flowstatus supplies all active team members, their live activity, all task assignments/dependencies/attempts/outputs, captain mail and quality/coverage state to the captain&#39;s model context. - Current-control decision scope: captain model chooses new/reassigned current member commitments, can halt or resume this team, send corrective instructions and change the task graph based on the complete status/failure feedback. - Disturbance / variety regulated: concurrent or stuck members, stale attempts, work failures, dependency bottlenecks, incomplete coverage, quality escalation and changing current priorities within an approved goal. - Decisive decision or feedback right: captain model can see all members/task statuses/mailboxes and choose current task creation, assignment/reassignment, messages, halt/resume and follow-up based on returned work. Human approval of the initial staged plan is a bounded launch right, not automatically a distinct S3 parent mode. - Decision owner: the model-backed captain session for semantic whole-team current interventions; deterministic scheduler selects ready tasks and enforces locks/attempt identity; operator chooses profile/approval policy and is the final deployment authority. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: flowstatus whole-team view, per-member flowsendmessage, flowreassigntask with interrupt, flowcreatetask, flowhalt/flowresume, membership controls, state/coverage diagnostics, event-driven scheduler and mailboxes. - Closure path: member progress/failure → persisted task state and captain-directed message (live steer or mailbox) → captain sees all task commitments and evidence in flowstatus → model chooses intervention/reassignment/repair/stop → gated tool writes state and scheduler dispatches changed work → subsequent member outcomes return again. - Boundary reachability: selected default subagents mode creates an actual captain-owned team, dispatches members and registers captain-facing tools; live message steering and durable fallback make the feedback available in normal supported operation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: scheduler/quality gates can enforce the captain&#39;s selected commitments, but cannot decide whether to change a goal-constrained priority, replace an implementer or ask for revised evidence. That discretionary current-control choice resides in the captain model when operated as a team. - Evidence: captain-only lifecycle tools, whole-team status, live member→captain steer, task ownership/interrupt and reassignment, scheduler feedback. - Basis: structural supported-mode control loop, with explicit tool rights and return path. - Confidence: medium-high for model-visible group view/control; no live provider captain intervention was measured. - Caveats: a pure manual runner is an operator editing a static plan, not autonomous S3. A single all-in-one worker task cannot be promoted to a whole-team S3 merely because a captain persona exists.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: obtain a separate reviewer model&#39;s judgment on implementation/repair claims using task files, acceptance evidence and changed paths, and route findings back to corrective control. - Disturbance / variety regulated: divergence between an implementer&#39;s completion assertion and raw code/test artifacts, including unresolved severe findings and incorrect acceptance or changed-path claims. - Claim being audited: an implementation or repair member&#39;s reported completion, scope compliance, tested behavior and asserted acceptance against the actual delivered artifacts. - Ordinary reporting path: implementer flowupdatetask status/output/changedPaths/acceptanceResults/commandsRun plus ordinary member message to the captain. - Complementary access path: a separately assigned review member receives a structured kind: review task with reviewedTaskId, independent prompt/work and access to code/verification artifacts under host task tools, then returns its own verdict and structured findings with concrete required fixes. - Independence boundary: in the selected structured quality mode, the reviewer is a different member model session from the implementer; automatic follow-up deliberately chooses a reviewer other than the repair implementer. This is task-relative independence, not an external security audit. - Decisive decision or feedback right: reviewer model inspects evidence and makes pass, needsrevision or reject judgment; pure rules validate the result shape and plan bounded repairs, while captain/user can act on escalations. - Decision owner: independent reviewer member model for the substantive audit judgment; constructor-owned evaluateQualityCompletion and planQualityFollowUp enforce nonfabrication of completion and deterministic follow-up limits; human/captain control task policies. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: structured review task kind/objective/acceptance, required verdict/findings with problem/requiredFix/optional file/line, task-specific claim and role permissions, changed-path/verification gates, repair/review round counters and team status/canvas. - Closure path: implementer output + actual code/evidence → separately executed reviewer task → model verdict with findings → flowupdatetask rejects unsupported pass or accepts failure → planQualityFollowUp creates scoped repair and fresh independent review (or escalates) → member performs repair and reviewer rechecks → captain sees updated current quality state. - Boundary reachability: standard flowcreatetask accepts review tasks, default active subagents runner dispatches them as ordinary member work, flowupdatetask carries verdict/findings and the first-party follow-up rule selects a distinct reviewer. This is selectable first-party functionality, not a standalone benchmark evaluator. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the evidence comparison and diagnosis is model-owned by a distinct reviewer, not produced solely by the implementer or a deterministic unit test. The shape validator and automatic repair planner do not confer audit judgment ownership. - Who acts on findings: an implementation member performs planned repair; another reviewer performs the new review; captain receives status/escalation and can reallocate current work or ask the user when limits are reached. - Evidence: review-task schema and real dispatch, review evidence parsing, completion review verdict checks, separate reviewer and repair round, review assignment prompt. - Basis: structurally evidenced selected-mode audit with model-authored reviewer verdict; not inferred from generic QA label. - Confidence: medium-high for path/role separation, medium for observed real-artifact review quality without live provider. - Caveats: the captain/profile must actually create/assign a separate reviewer task; ordinary work has no mandatory review. The initial schema does not universally forbid a captain from assigning an ordinary review to the same implementer, so S3=A refers to a distinct reviewer mode and explicit automatic follow-up separation, not every team or task.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no shipped whole-team, externally and prospectively oriented agent loop constructs future adaptation options and closes them against current S3 capacity. - Disturbance / variety regulated: current task progress, plan defects, implementation/test failures, member availability, required scope changes and review follow-ups; these are present goal/work controls. - Decisive decision or feedback right: captain model revises the current staged or active task plan; human/deployment author configures roles/profiles and decides whether the team should pursue a new goal. - Decision owner: captain for current work, operator/higher organization for long-horizon goals and profile evolution; no first-party future-environment intelligence owner found. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: profile registry, plan requirements, goal coverage, task DAG, status/coverage matrix, review findings and imported team sources. - Closure path: task failure/review updates current repair or work; no separate external trend/opportunity sensing → modeled alternatives → S3–S4 dialogue → changed long-term capability path is shipped. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: current-task planning and immediate code repair are not S4 even when model-driven; roster/configurability is not strategic external adaptation. - Evidence: profile/task-planning modes, bounded repair follow-up, captain tool boundary. - Basis: structural negative. - Confidence: medium-high across reviewed supported modes. - Caveats: the plugin can host a research task about future technologies, but a one-off task is an S1 operation until a governed prospective capability-change loop is evidenced.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no identity/ultimate-policy deliberation and returned governance path is established for the deployed team as an ongoing viable organization. - Disturbance / variety regulated: team membership, task ownership, profile review rules, host permissions, approval of staged plan and risk/quality limits are ordinary task controls. - Decisive decision or feedback right: human operator/deployment author chooses goals/profiles and approves a staged plan; captain model edits a plan within those granted rights; host identity/permission checks protect sessions. - Decision owner: human/external DSH host for ultimate organizational identity and policy; kernel enforces profiles, team session role and task capability. No explicit first-party S5 judgment at an ultimate identity tension is evidenced. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: flowapprove staging gate, captain/member access restrictions, persisted identity/team IDs, member retirement guard, review policy, scope and acceptance constraints. - Closure path: approved plan activates new member work and constraints, but that is normal task launch/current-control authorization, not a distinct identity-level issue routed to legitimate parent S5 and returned as ultimate policy. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: human approval of an ordinary plan and profile YAML do not automatically become parent-governed S5; a captain role label is not ultimate authority. - Evidence: staged approval and tool rights, captain/member tool distinction, retired member guard. - Basis: structural negative. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: a human organization using the plugin may provide real higher-recursion governance, but the plugin&#39;s task approval alone does not demonstrate the required S5 function.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/4d0c2bbcb966bc026eba64cf8652da2cc1922f17/assessments/ds/dsh-flow.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 4d0c2bbcb966bc026eba64cf8652da2cc1922f17\">4d0c2bb &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-02T00:29:12+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/HarnessDesk/HarnessDesk\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">HarnessDesk</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-01\">2026-10-01</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/HarnessDesk/HarnessDesk/tree/24add77185eebe321b0aa0b578933acf4a8e05d3\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 24add77185eebe321b0aa0b578933acf4a8e05d3\">24add77 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: perform repository-facing engineering work as a HarnessDesk-seated autonomous coding-agent cell and return changed artifacts, outcomes and evidence into the desk. - Disturbance / variety regulated: task-specific repository state, implementation alternatives, tool/test feedback, runtime capability differences, blockers and evidence encountered while producing a software outcome. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose and revise the next task-specific coding, inspection or tool action from the objective and returned environment state. - Decision owner: the supported autonomous coding-agent session seated through HarnessDesk, including a first-party Agent role instantiated on a compatible Codex/ACP runtime. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Agent briefs/seating, adapter negotiation, transcripts, plugin tools, approvals/permission ceilings, worktrees, task/context state and host persistence. - Closure path: task enters seated conversation → agent inspects/acts through its runtime → repository/tool feedback returns → same agent revises, continues or completes → HarnessDesk persists outcome/context/evidence. - Boundary reachability: Agent definitions, seating, adapters, Room membership and tool projection ship in the standard distribution; no application-authored orchestration is required to instantiate the supported worker cell. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the autonomous worker while leaving host state, worktrees, board and adapters leaves enforcement/persistence but no actor making the open-ended engineering decisions. - Evidence: README.md; docs/architecture.md; docs/agents.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: internal delegation/planning/memory of Codex, Claude, Cursor, Gemini or other external runtimes is not inherited.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: attenuate destructive interference among concurrent coding cells sharing tasks/files and prevent recursive peer-message chatter from destabilizing the Room. - Disturbance / variety regulated: overlapping edits or claims on the same task/path, unsafe concurrent work over shared repository state, and uncontrolled reciprocal agent messaging. - Distinct S1 units: two or more independently running HarnessDesk-seated coding-agent conversations in one Room/project, potentially on different vendor runtimes. - Inter-S1 disturbance: two cells can attempt the same card or overlapping files, or wake one another repeatedly through peer messages. - Attenuating coordination relation: atomic Board claims reject held intents, unfinished dependencies and overlapping file globs; leases expose stranded claims; worktrees isolate races; Channel delivery is attributed, rate/duplicate/queue limited and awakened replies are not forwarded back automatically. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: conflict/refusal/claim results are returned to the requesting agent, which chooses another task/path, waits, releases, communicates, uses isolation or escalates; dependency context is returned to later claimants. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the implementation targets concrete inter-worker collision modes—file/task contention and message oscillation—and closes explicit refusal/return paths that change later worker behaviour. - Decisive decision or feedback right: after host conflict feedback, autonomous participating agents choose the mutual-adjustment response; the host deterministically enforces exclusion and loop constraints. - Decision owner: participating autonomous coding-agent cells. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Board transactions, file-glob conflict checks, leases, worktrees, dependency gating, delivery states, loop guards, context packages and host audit state. - Closure path: agents claim intended work → host accepts or rejects incompatible ownership → result returns to agent → agent changes work/communication or proceeds → release/completion changes the shared state seen by peers. - Boundary reachability: Board/team tools are projected to supported Codex and ACP agents; host caller correlation rejects unattributed calls, so the coordination loop is available without custom external glue. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic exclusion could still block a conflict without the agent, but it would not choose the organizational response to that conflict. That discretion is held by the autonomous participant. - Evidence: docs/multi-agent.md; docs/architecture.md; README.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: claim enforcement regulates supported Board/worktree participants, not unrelated out-of-band processes.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: C(P) - Function: regulate the current Room as a whole by choosing roles/permissions, moving current work between cells, reacting to round outcomes and reserving irreversible steps for a selected authority. - Disturbance / variety regulated: current mismatch among work cells—who should perform which role, whether rejected work returns to implementation, what a seat may publish/merge, and when current work must stop, change owner or reach a person. - Whole-system current view: Room Board/roster/channel and Flow run expose current members, claims, role-addressed cards, outcomes and run state across the participating S1 cells. - Current-control decision scope: whole-Room allocation, role/permission assignment, guarded transitions between current work rounds, intervention on blocked/rejected work and ownership of irreversible merge-like actions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: select/revise those current roles, commitments and transitions on behalf of the Room rather than merely execute one local task. - Decision owner: base constructor mode—developer/Flow author chooses the policy and deterministic Flow engine enforces it; parent mode—human Room referee owns current assignment/intervention/irreversible-step choices. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Flow parser/validator, role seating, permission ceilings, outcome vocabulary, guarded first-match transitions, frozen run policy, Board state, person-role cards and stop/restart semantics. - Closure path: Room/round state reaches predeclared Flow policy or human referee → current-control decision selects next role/card/permission/intervention → Flow/Board machinery changes subsequent S1 work. - Boundary reachability: Flow is a supported first-party project format with runnable shipped examples; ordinary Rooms expose the human referee path directly in the local standard distribution. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the Flow engine enforces a preselected whole-Room policy but does not autonomously choose/revise that policy from current variety; ordinary Room documentation explicitly assigns the referee right to the human. No autonomous S3 supervisor is established. - Evidence: docs/flows.md; docs/multi-agent.md; .harnessdesk/flows/fix-and-review.yml. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: deterministic transitions do not own S3 discretion, and a reviewer/judge is not promoted to S3 merely because it controls one edge.\">C(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: challenge an implementation cell&#39;s ordinary completion claim through independent autonomous inspection of primary artifacts and return findings into corrective work. - Disturbance / variety regulated: uncertainty that the fixer&#39;s own completion/publication claim corresponds to a correct change rather than merely a plausible self-report. - Claim being audited: that a published fixer change is correct and suitable to proceed. - Ordinary reporting path: fixer publishes its own card, note/context and may report checks or success. - Complementary access path: shipped fix-and-review opens three separate reviewer-agent seats; each is ordered to inspect the pull-request diff and surrounding code independently and not read other reviewers&#39; answers. - Independence boundary: reviewers are distinct autonomous seats from the fixer, receive the produced change rather than the fixer&#39;s hidden reasoning, and are explicitly isolated from one another&#39;s judgments for the review round. - Who acts on findings: the Flow returns any request-changes findings to the fixer, which must answer them and republish; unanimous approval alone advances to the human merge step. - Decisive decision or feedback right: judge from independent artifact inspection whether the implementation claim withstands review and identify defects requiring correction. - Decision owner: the autonomous reviewer agents own approve versus request-changes; the deterministic engine only validates/collects outcomes and applies the return rule. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: reviewer role seating, addressed cards, outcome validation, context packages, Flow rules, check/diff/PR/CI evidence, commit-bound freshness and stale-evidence downgrading. - Closure path: fixer publishes → independent reviewers inspect raw artifacts → judgments/findings return → any negative finding reopens fixer work → corrected publication is reviewed again. - Boundary reachability: fix-and-review.yml is committed as a runnable HarnessDesk Flow and standard Agent/seat/Flow machinery opens the reviewer actors locally. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing reviewer agents while retaining deterministic Flow/evidence machinery leaves no actor making the independent semantic judgment; check/CI facts support rather than replace that judgment. - Evidence: .harnessdesk/flows/fix-and-review.yml; docs/flows.md; docs/multi-agent.md; docs/agents.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: this is a supported Flow mode, not a claim that every ordinary Room automatically audits every S1 result.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no material first-party external-and-prospective adaptation loop is established in the reviewed standard distribution. - Disturbance / variety regulated: possible future changes in models, runtimes, user needs, CI events, schedules, hosted execution and tool capabilities were inspected. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established that turns sensed external/future distinctions into adaptation options and returns an adaptation choice into current HarnessDesk capability. - Decision owner: none established for S4 at the deployed boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: runtime capability negotiation, local runtime/version discovery, usage dashboards, repository VISION/roadmap and current Flow/Room state. - Closure path: no supported outside-and-then adaptation decision-and-return loop exists at the frozen ref. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: current capability discovery and runtime selection react to present machine state; they do not model a future environment and develop adaptation options. Roadmap/market reasoning is adjacent development governance. - Evidence: docs/architecture.md; VISION.md; docs/data-boundaries.md; docs/flows.md. - Basis: explicit + structural absence. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: PR/CI/timer/webhook-triggered and hosted work are named as future directions, but current Flow triggers are manual and those lanes are not built.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no material runtime identity/ultimate-policy closure is established for the selected HarnessDesk project/Room organization. - Disturbance / variety regulated: possible tension over ultimate organizational purpose, identity, trust boundary or policy requiring highest-level closure was inspected. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established beyond ordinary permission ceilings, approvals, Flow policy, local data settings and current-control decisions. - Decision owner: none established for S5 at the reviewed deployed boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Agent briefs/permission ceilings, common approval policy, plugin permission gates, human referee, Flow files, local-only data defaults, VISION promises and settings. - Closure path: no first-party runtime path was found where an identity/ultimate-policy issue reaches legitimate ultimate authority and the returned decision governs subsequent operation as S5. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: agents do not own ultimate HarnessDesk identity/policy, but absence of agent ownership does not create parent S5; ordinary approval, merge, role permission and Flow edit remain operational/current-control matters. - Evidence: docs/agents.md; docs/flows.md; docs/multi-agent.md; VISION.md; docs/data-boundaries.md. - Basis: explicit + structural absence. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: public promises such as vendor neutrality/local-data defaults are maintainer/development governance surfaces and are not borrowed as a deployed Room&#39;s S5 owner.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/3f74e33b03b91b79ed7e360fbc1eae4d60dcf2c4/assessments/harnessdesk.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 3f74e33b03b91b79ed7e360fbc1eae4d60dcf2c4\">3f74e33 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-24T01:05:54+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Gurp-S/XEYO\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">XEYO</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-23\">2026-10-23</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Gurp-S/XEYO/tree/5a41ffb430549e6871e09f81be26f41299b423d8\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 5a41ffb430549e6871e09f81be26f41299b423d8\">5a41ffb &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: investigate and modify a workspace in pursuit of a user task using model-selected tools and feedback. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing file content, differing user requests, tool errors, refused permissions, process failures, stale work and uncertain task solutions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose subsequent Read/Grep/Glob/Edit/Write/Bash and other supported operations after tool results, or choose delegation and synthesis. - Decision owner: parent model through queryloop, and independent model loops in runsubagent / worktree task sessions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: tool registry, permission checks, query-engine budget/session lifecycle, sidechain journal and deterministic write limits. - Closure path: task and tool observations → model chooses an operation → first-party registry executes or rejects it → factual tool result updates next model turn. - Boundary reachability: SessionPool builds the standard registry and model runtime; AgentTool creates child query loops. Separate coord worktree runs use sessionworkfn only on explicitly invoked CLI execution. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: host code restricts execution, but it does not select task-specific coding strategies or repair actions independently of the model. - Evidence: queryloop.py; queryengine.py; catalog.py. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: model providers are external inference dependencies and were not exercised in this assessment; Python module compilation does not establish live agent task success.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: regulate concurrent or overlapping task and workspace commitments among distinct child/root S1 actors. - Disturbance / variety regulated: simultaneous child writes, stale read versions, conflicting file claims, clashing delegated scopes, and concurrent worktree task claims. - Distinct S1 units: separate child runsubagent query loops under one parent, each with its own context, task and abort token; additionally, independently executed coord worktree task sessions in the explicit CLI mode. - Inter-S1 disturbance: child and parent edits can target the same file; WriteStore serializes writes, checks content versions and returns stale/conflict outcomes, while optional coord.Planner denies claims intersecting active file scope leases. - Attenuating coordination relation: root and child write tools share a per-workspace WriteStore with per-file serialization and read-base hash validation; explicit coord tasks additionally acquire scope leases before a compare-and-swap claim. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: tool failures, current-version conflicts and child outcomes return into model-visible tool results. A child can re-read and retry; the parent can repartition the remaining work after observing its child handoff. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: independently deciding child S1s can submit conflicting edits, and the shared write and lease checks reject those collisions with concrete state feedback. A simple spawn list or notification bus alone would not demonstrate this mutual interference attenuation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the parent model specifies division of work and child scopes and can revise work after observed outcomes; child models respond to rejected changes. Deterministic guards arbitrate individual file/lease races, not the purpose of all task allocation. - Decision owner: model-backed parent/children for task-scoped reallocation and conflict response, with host-owned CAS/lease gates for safe execution. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: AgentTool.scope, shared WriteStore, child registry and result feedback, coord.Planner.claimnext scope lease followed by task CAS, WorkerPool and Reconciler. - Closure path: concurrent task assignment → child proposes workspace action → content hash/scope lease checks accept or return conflict → model obtains factual outcome and may retry/repartition; in coord mode tasks are reopened or merged with recorded status. - Boundary reachability: registered Agent and shared WriteStore are wired into server sessions, and coord run is a shipped explicit CLI path guarded by coord.workers (default enabled for that CLI entry only). - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a shared execution mutex alone would be C; here independently deciding model workers and a model-facing delegation/result path supply an actual S1-to-S1 operational feedback loop. A default coord flag does not imply that all ordinary sessions enter the worktree planner. - Evidence: agenttool.py; writestore.py; coord/planner.py; tests/coord/testorchestration.py. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: scope protection concerns first-party write tools, not arbitrary subprocess file mutation, and coord&#39;s automatic claim order is deterministic rather than a free-standing model planner.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A(P) - Function: maintain present control of the parent-and-child coding organization by assigning work, interpreting returned child outcomes, and changing descendant commitments. - Disturbance / variety regulated: child failures or cancellations, incomplete or obsolete subtasks, changed scope, exhausted child resources, stalled child action and user revisions during an active session. - Whole-system current view: parent receives each delegated child&#39;s terminal status, scoped file results and summarized handoff in its model context; server session endpoints expose the same current parent-session child roster, per-child sidechain and inbox/cancel status to the authorized operator. - Current-control decision scope: parent model chooses child tasks, bounded write scopes and follow-on tasks in response to a current cohort&#39;s reports; the human can inspect, abort and queue follow-up for a specific live child through the first-party server route. - Decisive decision or feedback right: assign and scope child work through the model-visible Agent tool, inspect returned child conclusions and decide follow-on allocations; in the distinct parent mode, inspect individual child transcripts and target running agents with cancel/follow-up/retry. - Decision owner: main model for model-mediated task allocation and later response; legitimate human session operator for the server&#39;s direct team-control endpoints (P). The deterministic tool dispatcher and UI cards do not themselves determine the purpose of allocations. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: AgentTool task description/scope/role, distinct child result messages, liveagents status and targeted abort/inbox, sidechain metadata, server /v1/sessions/{sessionid}/agents control routes. - Closure path: parent model sends child commitments → independent children execute/report → parent model incorporates reports into new assignments, or human inspects a live sidechain and issues a targeted command → first-party runtime applies it and exposes updated child state. - Boundary reachability: SessionPool wires AgentTool into ordinary server sessions; supported session routes list child status, provide transcript and implement actual per-child cancel and follow-up. Ended-child follow-up persists pending instructions for retry. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the main model decides meaningful task allocation from current child feedback, not just fixed concurrency limits. Human mode actually changes a live child process rather than merely displaying historical cards. - Evidence: agenttool.py; subagentrunner.py; routers/sessions.py; tests/testmultiagentp2agentops.py. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: the main model generally resumes after its synchronous Agent call returns, not during an active child tool call; interactive running-child commands shown here are human-owned, queued follow-ups are consumed at settle, and MAXDEPTH=1 excludes an unlimited agent hierarchy.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no independent in-product assessment of an implementer&#39;s success claim with a distinct evidence path and enforced correction return was established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: a child/model could claim a task is complete without correct code; the repository includes syntactic and lifecycle observations, but none is shown as an independent task-completion audit controller. - Claim being audited: prospective child final conclusions or merged task-worktree results; neither undergoes a first-party independently assigned reviewer check in the actually composed main session or coord run path. - Ordinary reporting path: child conclusion and structured ToolResult, progress/memory handoff, worker submission and reconciliation status. - Complementary access path: runtimeverification.verifyruntime can separately detect unmatched tool events, pending journal actions and missing cwd, but the query engine records it after the loop as a nonblocking diagnostic. coord.Reviewer.reject validates caller-provided findings but is not invoked by the live coord-run loop to generate those findings. - Independence boundary: diagnostics consult some underlying runtime facts, but the report cannot override the model result. A separately named reviewer class without a first-party invoking and adjudicating actor is not an actual independent audit loop. - Who acts on findings: an external caller could choose to pass findings to Reviewer; no automatic implementer-claim → independent judgment → changed completion/correction feedback loop is wired at this ref. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no independently exercised right to deny a claimed successful response on semantic or prescribed evidential grounds in the shipping path. - Decision owner: none established; user-supplied findings or developer tests are outside this agent organization&#39;s independently deciding complement. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: passive runtimeverification, coord.Reviewer.reject, required structured findings and scope-subset enforcement, WriteStore incremental syntax checks. - Closure path: diagnostics → stored report without changed model answer; external findings → optional standalone Reviewer.reject can reopen a task. Neither closes a first-party independent audit path in active operations. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: testing basic syntax, enforcing version freshness, or checking a provided finding is execution integrity/coordination, not a sufficiently independent complementary claim challenge. - Evidence: runtimeverification.py; queryengine.py; coord/reviewer.py; cli/coordcmd.py. - Basis: explicit + bounded structural absence review. - Confidence: medium-high for bounded negative. - Caveats: external integrators could supply an independent reviewer and wire it to Reviewer.reject; this is a possible composition, not observed first-party S3 closure.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no first-party long-horizon external scan, future capability choice, and strategic change returned to the present XEYO organization is shown. - Disturbance / variety regulated: web/model information, plugins and memory can improve current work, but do not instantiate an organizational outside/future regulator. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no distinct prospective first-party strategy revision right identified. - Decision owner: user/project maintainers configure future models and features; individual coding model chooses task-local tools and research. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: web search and memory tools, session compaction and model choice, optional coord DAG and development G0–G6 stages. - Closure path: model can use external facts to solve the current task; no demonstrated diagnosis of changing future organizational environment → adaptation option → binding S3 change. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: prospective product development outside the running agent and present-task research are not the same as operational S4. - Evidence: python/ARCHITECTURE.md; python/coord/planner.py; python/engine/queryloop.py. - Basis: bounded structural absence review. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: a model can formulate a plan within a software project; this is task planning unless it demonstrably changes the organization&#39;s future capability allocation.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no independent top-level mission/identity settlement with binding feedback into the runtime was established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: tool permissions, user intervention, child scope, model instructions and fixed resource budgets constrain actions but do not create an organization-level policy/identity adjudication issue. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no in-system final policy authority over the organization&#39;s purpose is represented. - Decision owner: users set their tasks, approvals and follow-ups; developers define default capabilities and rules. Neither is shown as a distinct in-runtime constitutional feedback function. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: permission gates, agenttype prompts, hard MAXDEPTH, write scopes, user cancellation, workspace-level settings and developer instructions. - Closure path: operation requested → permission/limit allows or rejects individual tool use; no ultimate-identity dispute → legitimate judgment → revised governing policy return path exists. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: operating constraints and legitimate human authority over an assigned coding task do not prove an ongoing S5 identity-setting institution. - Evidence: python/engine/agentroles.py; python/permissions/writescope.py; python/tools/agenttool/agenttool.py. - Basis: bounded structural absence review. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: neither the user&#39;s absolute ability to stop a session nor repo-owner contributor policies may be silently promoted to operational S5=P.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/bbfbb3843aa0fd4058bbb956c2605fbb0971f5ff/assessments/xe/xeyo.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit bbfbb3843aa0fd4058bbb956c2605fbb0971f5ff\">bbfbb38 &#8599;</a></td></tr>"];
